(function(){(window.PhysicaNeetYear=window.PhysicaNeetYear||{})[2020]=[{id:"neet-2020-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Transport of oxygen",q:"Identify the wrong statement with reference to transport of oxygen.",o:["Partial pressure of CO2 can interfere with O2 binding with haemoglobin","Higher H+ conc. in alveoli favours the formation of oxyhaemoglobin","Low pCO2 in alveoli favours the formation of oxyhaemoglobin","Binding of oxygen with haemoglobin is mainly related to partial pressure of O2"],c:1,s:`Oxygen binds haemoglobin more when pO\u2082 is high, pCO\u2082 is low and H\u207A is low (alveoli).
So statement (2) is wrong: high H\u207A would lower the binding, not favour oxyhaemoglobin.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2020-q002",pn:2,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Adaptive radiation and human impact",q:"Which of the following refer to correct example(s) of organisms which have evolved due to changes in environment brought about by anthropogenic action ? (a) Darwin\u2019s Finches of Galapagos islands (b) Herbicide resistant weeds (c) Drug resistant eukaryotes (d) Man-created breeds of domesticated animals like dogs",o:["(a) and (c)","(b), (c) and (d)","only (d)","only (a)"],c:1,s:`Evolution due to human action: herbicide-resistant weeds, drug-resistant eukaryotes and man-made dog breeds.
Darwin's finches evolved by natural causes, not by human action.
So (b), (c) and (d).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2020-q003",pn:3,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Seed dormancy",q:"Which of the following is not an inhibitory substance governing seed dormancy ?",o:["Abscisic acid","Phenolic acid","Para-ascorbic acid","Gibberellic acid"],c:3,s:`Abscisic acid, phenolic acids and para-ascorbic acid inhibit germination and keep seeds dormant.
Gibberellic acid promotes germination, so it is not an inhibitor.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2020-q004",pn:4,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Common diseases and pathogens",q:`Match the following diseases with the causative organism and select the correct option.
(a) Typhoid  (i) Wuchereria
(b) Pneumonia  (ii) Plasmodium
(c) Filariasis  (iii) Salmonella
(d) Malaria  (iv) Haemophilus`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`Typhoid - Salmonella (iii).
Pneumonia - Haemophilus (iv).
Filariasis - Wuchereria (i).
Malaria - Plasmodium (ii).
So (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2020-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases",st:"Mechanism of breathing",q:`Select the correct events that occur during inspiration.
(a) Contraction of diaphragm
(b) Contraction of external inter-coastal muscles
(c) Pulmonary volume decreases
(d) Intra pulmonary pressure increases`,o:["(c) and (d)","(a), (b) and (d)","only (d)","(a) and (b)"],c:3,s:`Inspiration: diaphragm and external intercostal muscles contract, the thoracic volume rises.
Pulmonary volume increases and intrapulmonary pressure falls below atmospheric, so air enters.
So (a) and (b) are correct; (c) and (d) are wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Breathing and Exchange of Gases"},{id:"neet-2020-q006",pn:6,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Photorespiration",q:"The oxygenation activity of RuBisCo enzyme in photorespiration leads to the formation of :",o:["1 molecule of 3-C compound","1 molecule of 6-C compound","1 molecule of 4-C compound and 1 molecule of 2-C compound","2 molecules of 3-C compound"],c:0,s:`In oxygenation RuBisCO adds O\u2082 to RuBP.
This gives one 3-C compound (PGA) and one 2-C compound (phosphoglycolate).
The printed key (1) is kept.
Note: Printed answer 1; oxygenation actually gives one 3-C and one 2-C molecule, and none of the options states this fully.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q007",pn:7,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants",st:"Electron transport chain",q:"In light reaction, plastoquinone facilitates the transfer of electrons from :",o:["Cytb6f complex to PS-I","PS-I to NADP+","PS-I to ATP synthase","PS-II to Cytb6f complex"],c:3,s:`Electrons leave PS-II and are accepted by plastoquinone.
Plastoquinone passes them to the Cytb\u2086f complex.
So it carries electrons from PS-II to Cytb\u2086f.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Photosynthesis in Higher Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q008",pn:8,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Gel electrophoresis",q:"In gel electrophoresis, separated DNA fragments can be visualized with the help of :",o:["Ethidium bromide in UV radiation","Acetocarmine in UV radiation","Ethidium bromide in infrared radiation","Acetocarmine in bright blue light"],c:0,s:`DNA fragments in the gel are stained with ethidium bromide.
Under UV light the stained DNA shows as orange bands.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"ECG",q:"The QRS complex in a standard ECG represents :",o:["Depolarisation of auricles","Depolarisation of ventricles","Repolarisation of ventricles","Repolarisation of auricles"],c:1,s:`In an ECG the P wave is atrial depolarisation.
The QRS complex is ventricular depolarisation.
The T wave is ventricular repolarisation.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2020-q010",pn:10,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Alternation of generations",q:`The plant parts which consist of two generations-one within the other:
(a) Pollen grains inside the anther
(b) Germinated pollen grain with two male gametes
(c) Seed inside the fruit
(d) Embryo sac inside the ovule`,o:["(a), (b) & (c)","(c) & (d)","(a) & (d)","(a) only"],c:2,s:`A pollen grain inside the anther holds the male gametophyte within the sporophyte anther.
An embryo sac inside the ovule holds the female gametophyte within the sporophyte ovule.
So (a) and (d) have two generations one within the other.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Malaria",q:"The infectious stage of Plasmodium that enters the human body is:",o:["Sporozoites","Female gametocytes","Male gametocytes","Trophozoites"],c:0,s:"The sporozoite is the infective stage injected by the female Anopheles mosquito into humans.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2020-q012",pn:12,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Heartwood and sapwood",q:"Identify the incorrect statement.",o:["Sapwood is involved in conduction of water and minerals from root to leaf.","Sapwood is the innermost secondary xylem and is lighter in colour.","Due to deposition of tannins, resins, oils etc. heart wood is dark in colour.","heart wood does not conduct water but gives mechanical support."],c:1,s:`Sapwood is the outer, younger, lighter secondary xylem and conducts water.
Heartwood is the innermost, dark xylem and gives support.
So the statement that sapwood is the innermost xylem is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Convergent evolution",q:"Flippers of Penguins and Dolphins are examples of:",o:["Convergent evolution","Industrial melanism","Natural selection","Adaptive radiation"],c:0,s:`Penguin and dolphin flippers look alike and do the same job but have different origins.
This is convergent evolution (analogous organs).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2020-q014",pn:14,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"ABO blood groups",q:"Identify the wrong statement with reference to the gene 'I' that controls ABO blood groups.",o:["A person will have only two of the three alleles.","When IA and IB are present together they express same type of sugar.","Allele 'i' does not produce any sugar.","The gene (I) has three alleles."],c:1,s:`Gene I has three alleles IA, IB and i; a person has only two.
IA and IB together are codominant: each makes its own different sugar.
So the statement that they make the same sugar is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2020-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Phylum Chordata",q:`Which of the following statements are true for the phylum-Chordata?
(a) In Urochordata notochord extends from head to tail and it is present throughout their life
(b) In Vertebrata notochord is present during the embryonic period only
(c) Central nervous system is dorsal and hollow
(d) Chordata is divided into 3 subphyla : Hemichordata, Tunicata and Cephalochordata`,o:["(c) and (a)","(a) and (b)","(b) and (c)","(d) and (c)"],c:2,s:`(b) In vertebrates the notochord is present only in the embryo and replaced by the vertebral column - true.
(c) The CNS is dorsal and hollow - true.
(a) is wrong because in Urochordata the notochord is only in the larval tail.
(d) is wrong because the subphyla are Urochordata, Cephalochordata and Vertebrata.
So (b) and (c).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q016",pn:16,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Disorders of kidney",q:"Presence of which of the following conditions in urine are indicative of Diabetes Mellitus ?",o:["Uremia and Renal Calculi","Ketonuria and Glycosuria","Renal calculi and Hyperglycaemia","Uremia and Ketonuria"],c:1,s:`In diabetes mellitus there is high blood glucose.
Glucose appears in urine (glycosuria) and fat breakdown gives ketone bodies in urine (ketonuria).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2020-q017",pn:17,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Translation",q:"The first phase of translation is",o:["Recognition of DNA molecule","Aminoacylation of tRNA","Recognition of an anti-codom","Binding of mRNA to ribosome"],c:3,s:`Translation starts with the binding of mRNA to the small ribosomal subunit.
The printed key (4) is kept.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2020-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Inflorescence and ovary position",q:"Rayflorets have",o:["Superior ovary","Hypogynous ovary","Half inferiour ovary","Inferior ovary"],c:3,s:"Ray florets of sunflower (Asteraceae) have an inferior ovary.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Growth phases",q:"The process of growth is maximum during",o:["Lag phase","Senescence","Dormancy","Log phase"],c:3,s:"The exponential (log) phase shows the maximum growth rate.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2020-q020",pn:20,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Types of roots",q:"The roots that originate from the base of the stem are",o:["Primary roots","Prop roots","Lateral roots","Fibrous roots"],c:3,s:`In monocots the primary root is short-lived.
A cluster of roots arises from the base of the stem; these are fibrous roots.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination agents",q:"In water hyacinth and water lily, pollination takes place by",o:["water currents only","wind and water","insects and water","insects or wind"],c:3,s:`Water hyacinth and water lily are mostly pollinated by insects or wind.
Water pollination is rare in them.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q022",pn:22,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Sewage treatment",q:"Which of the following is put into anaerobic sludge digester for further sewage treatment ?",o:["Floating debris","Effluents of primary treatment","Activated sludge","Primary sludge"],c:2,s:`After secondary treatment the flocs are settled as activated sludge.
A part is returned as inoculum and the rest goes to anaerobic sludge digesters.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2020-q023",pn:23,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Body symmetry and coelom",q:"Bilaterally symmetrical and acoelomate animals are exemplified by",o:["Platyhelminthes","Aschelminthes","Annelida","Ctenophora"],c:0,s:`Platyhelminthes are bilaterally symmetrical and acoelomate.
Aschelminthes are pseudocoelomate, Annelida coelomate and Ctenophora radial.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q024",pn:24,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Amino acids",q:"Identify the basic amino acid fro the following",o:["Glutamic Acid","Lysine","Valine","Tyrosine"],c:1,s:`Lysine has an extra amino group in the side chain, so it is a basic amino acid.
Glutamic acid is acidic; valine is neutral; tyrosine is aromatic.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2020-q025",pn:25,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Assisted reproductive technologies",q:"In which of the following techniques, the embryos are transferred to assist those females who cannot conceive ?",o:["GIFT and ZIFT","ICSI and ZIFT","GIFT and ICSI","ZIFT and IUT"],c:3,s:`In ZIFT the zygote or early embryo (up to 8 blastomeres) is moved into the fallopian tube.
In IUT embryos with more than 8 blastomeres are moved into the uterus.
So the embryo-transfer methods are ZIFT and IUT.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2020-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Inclusion bodies",q:"Which of the following statements about inclusion bodies in incorrect ?",o:["These are involved in ingestion of food particles","They line free in the cytoplasm","These represent reserve material in cytoplasm","They are not bound any membrane"],c:0,s:`Inclusion bodies are reserve material lying free in the cytoplasm without a membrane.
They do not ingest food, so statement (1) is wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2020-q027",pn:27,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Chromosomal theory of inheritance",q:"Experimental verification of the chromosomal theory of inheritance was done by -",o:["Sutton","Boveri","Morgan","Mendel"],c:2,s:`Sutton and Boveri proposed the theory.
Morgan gave its experimental verification using Drosophila.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2020-q028",pn:28,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Reproductive Health",st:"Sexually transmitted infections",q:"Select the option including all sexually transmitted diseases.",o:["Gonorrhoea, Malaria, Genital herpes","AIDS, Malaria, Filaria","Cancer, AIDS, Syphilis","Gonorrhoea, Syphilis, Genital herpes"],c:3,s:`Gonorrhoea, syphilis and genital herpes are STDs.
Malaria and filaria are vector-borne and cancer is not infectious.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Reproductive Health"},{id:"neet-2020-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Insulin",q:"Which of the following statements is not correct ?",o:["The proinsulin has an extra peptide called C-peptide","The functional insulin has A and B chains linked together by hydrogen bonds","Genetically engineered insulin is produced in E-coli","In man insulin is synthesised as a proinsulin"],c:1,s:`Mature insulin has chains A and B joined by disulphide bonds, not hydrogen bonds.
So statement (2) is not correct.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2020-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell: The Unit of Life",st:"Golgi apparatus",q:"Which is the important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids in eukaryotic cells ?",o:["Peroxisomes","Golgi bodies","Polysomes","Endoplasmic reticulum"],c:1,s:"The Golgi apparatus is the important site of formation of glycoproteins and glycolipids.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell: The Unit of Life"},{id:"neet-2020-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Microbes in industrial products",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option.
(a) Clostridium butylicum  (i) Cyclosporin-A
(b) Trichoderma polysporum  (ii) Butyric Acid
(c) Monascus  (iii) Citric Acid
(d) Aspergillus niger  (iv) Blood cholesterol lowering agent`,o:["(a) \uF0AE (ii) ; (b) \uF0AE (i) ; (c) \uF0AE (iv) ; (d) \uF0AE (iii)","(a) \uF0AE (i) ; (b) \uF0AE (ii) ; (c) \uF0AE (iv) ; (d) \uF0AE (iii)","(a) \uF0AE (iv) ; (b) \uF0AE (iii) ; (c) \uF0AE (ii) ; (d) \uF0AE (i)","(a) \uF0AE (iii) ; (b) \uF0AE (iv) ; (c) \uF0AE (ii) ; (d) \uF0AE (i)"],c:0,s:`Clostridium butylicum - butyric acid.
Trichoderma polysporum - cyclosporin A.
Monascus - blood cholesterol lowering agent.
Aspergillus niger - citric acid.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2020-q032",pn:32,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Evidences for evolution",q:"Embryological support for evolution was disapproved by :",o:["Alfred Wallace","Charles Darwin","Oparin","Karl Ernst von Baer"],c:3,s:"Karl Ernst von Baer noted that embryos of different animals do not show the adult features of others, so he disproved the embryological support.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2020-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Cloning vector",q:"The sequence that controls the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector, is termed :",o:["Ori site","Palindromic sequence","Recognition site","Selectable marker"],c:0,s:"The origin of replication (ori) starts replication and controls the copy number of the linked DNA.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q034",pn:34,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Viruses and viroids",q:"Which of the following is correct about viroids ?",o:["They have free RNA without protein coat.","They have DNA with protein coat.","They have free DNA without protein coat.","They have RNA with protein coat."],c:0,s:`Viroids are free RNA without a protein coat.
They are smaller than viruses.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2020-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Environmental protocols",q:"Montreal protocol was signed in 1987 for control of :",o:["Emission of ozone depleting substances","Release of Green House gases","Disposal of e-wastes","Transport of Genetically modified organisms from one country to another"],c:0,s:`The Montreal Protocol (1987) controls the emission of ozone depleting substances.
Note: The paper has no environmental issues chapter; nearest chapter used.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2020-q036",pn:36,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Respiration in Plants",st:"Krebs cycle",q:"The number of substrate level phosphorylations in one turn of citric acid cycle is :",o:["One","Two","Three","Zero"],c:0,s:`In one turn of the citric acid cycle, succinyl-CoA to succinate forms one GTP/ATP by substrate level phosphorylation.
So the number is one.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Respiration in Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Menstrual cycle hormones",q:"Which of the following hormone levels will cause release of ovum (ovulation) from the graffian follicle?",o:["High concentration of Progesterone","Low concentration of LH","Low concentration of FSH","High concentration of Estrongen"],c:3,s:`A surge of LH, triggered by high oestrogen, causes ovulation from the Graafian follicle.
So high oestrogen is the answer.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2020-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendelian disorders",q:"Select the correct match.",o:["Phenylketonuria \u2013 Autosomal dominant trait","Sickel cell anaemia \u2013 Autosomal recessive trait, chromosome-11","Thalassemia \u2013 X linked","Haemophilia \u2013 Y linked"],c:1,s:`Sickle cell anaemia is autosomal recessive, on chromosome 11.
Phenylketonuria is autosomal recessive, thalassemia autosomal and haemophilia X-linked.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2020-q039",pn:39,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"Cuboidal epithelium with brush border of microvilli is found in :",o:["ducts of salivary glands","proximal convoluted tubule of nephron","eustachian tube","lining of intestine"],c:1,s:"Cuboidal epithelium with a brush border of microvilli lines the proximal convoluted tubule of the nephron.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2020-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Ozone depletion",q:"Snow-blindness in Antarctic region is due to :",o:["Inflammation of cornea due to high dose of UV-B radiation","High reflection of light from snow","Damage to retina caused by infra-red rays","Freezing of fluids in the eye by low temperature"],c:0,s:`Ozone depletion lets more UV-B reach the earth.
UV-B inflames the cornea; in snow regions this is snow-blindness.
Note: The paper has no environmental issues chapter; nearest chapter used.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2020-q041",pn:41,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biological Classification",st:"Unicellular algae",q:"Which of the following pairs is of unicellular algae?",o:["Gelidium and Gracilaria","Anabaena and Volvox","Chlorella and Spirulina","Laminaria and Sargassum"],c:2,s:`Chlorella is a unicellular green alga and Spirulina is a unicellular blue-green alga.
Gelidium, Gracilaria, Volvox, Laminaria and Sargassum are multicellular or colonial.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biological Classification"},{id:"neet-2020-q042",pn:42,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants",st:"Monocot stem",q:"The transverse section of a plant shows following anatomical features: (a) Large number of scattered vascular bundles surrounded by bundle sheath. (b) Large conspicuous parenchymatous ground tissue. (c) Vascular bundles conjoint and closed. (d) Phloem parenchyma absent. Identify the category of plant and its part:",o:["Monocotyledonous root","Dicotyledonous stem","Dicotyledonous root","Monocotyledonous stem"],c:3,s:"Scattered, conjoint, closed vascular bundles with sheath, large parenchyma ground tissue and no phloem parenchyma are features of a monocot stem.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Anatomy of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q043",pn:43,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation",st:"Mendel's experiments",q:"How many true breeding pea plant varieties did Mendel select as pairs, which were similar except in one character with contrasting traits?",o:["2","14","8","4"],c:1,s:"Mendel took 14 true-breeding pea varieties, as seven pairs differing in one contrasting character each.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Principles of Inheritance and Variation"},{id:"neet-2020-q044",pn:44,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Red algae",q:"Floridean starch has structure similar to:",o:["Amylopectin and glycogen","Mannitol and algin","Laminarin and cellulose","Starch and cellulose"],c:0,s:`Floridean starch is the food reserve of red algae.
Its structure is like amylopectin and glycogen.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q045",pn:45,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Interphase",q:"Identify the correct statement with regard to G1 phase (Gap 1) of interphase.",o:["Reorganisation of all cell components takes place.","Cell is metabolically active, grows but does not replicate its DNA.","Nuclear Division takes place.","DNA synthesis or replication takes place."],c:1,s:`In G\u2081 the cell is metabolically active and grows but does not replicate DNA.
DNA synthesis is in S phase.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2020-q046",pn:46,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Microbes in Human Welfare",st:"Animal husbandry breeding",q:"By which method was a new breed 'Hisardale' of sheep formed by using Bikaneri ewes and Marino rams?",o:["Mutational breeding","Cross breeding","Inbreeding","Out crossing"],c:1,s:`Hisardale was formed by crossing Bikaneri ewes with Marino rams.
Crossing superior males of one breed with superior females of another is cross breeding.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Microbes in Human Welfare"},{id:"neet-2020-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Health and Disease",st:"Immunity",q:"Identify the wrong statement with reference to immunity.",o:['When ready-made antibodies are directly given, it is called "Passive immunity".',"Active immunity is quick and gives full response.","Foetus receives some antibodies from mother, it is an example for passive immunity.",`When exposed to antigen (living or dead) antibodies are produced in the host's body. It is called "Active immunity".`],c:1,s:`Active immunity is slow and takes time to give full response.
So the statement that it is quick is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Health and Disease"},{id:"neet-2020-q048",pn:48,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"The specific palindromic sequence which is recognized by EcoRI is :",o:["5'-GGAACC-3', 3'-CCTTGG-5'","5'-CTTAAG-3', 3'-GAATTC-5'","5'-GGATCC-3', 3-CCTAGG-5'","5'-GAATTC-3', 3'-CTTAAG-5'"],c:3,s:"EcoRI recognises the palindromic sequence 5'-GAATTC-3' with 3'-CTTAAG-5'.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA packaging",q:"If the distance between two consecutive base pairs is 0.34 nm and the total number of base pairs of a DNA double helix in a typical mammalian cell is 6.6 \xD7 10\u2079 bp, then the length of the DNA is approximately:",o:["2.5 meters","2.2 meters","2.7 meters","2.0 meters"],c:1,s:`Length = 6.6 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 0.34 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 m.
= 6.6 \xD7 0.34 m = 2.244 m.
This is about 2.2 metres.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2020-q050",pn:50,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Cockroach nervous system",q:"If the head of cockroach is removed, it may live for few days because:",o:["the cockroach does not have nervous system.","the head holds a small proportion of a nervous system while the rest is situated along the ventral part of its body.","the head holds a 1/3rd of a nervous system while the rest is situated along the dorsal part of its body.","the supra-oesophageal ganglia of the cockroach are situated in ventral part of abdomen."],c:1,s:`Only a small part of the nervous system (supra-oesophageal ganglia) is in the head.
The rest is along the ventral side of the body, so the body lives on for some days.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2020-q051",pn:51,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Trophic levels",q:`Match the trophic levels with their correct species examples in grassland ecosystem.
(a) Fourth trophic level  (i) Crow
(b) Second trophic level  (ii) Vulture
(c) First trophic level  (iii) Rabbit
(d) Third trophic level  (iv) Grass
Select the correct option :`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Grass is the first trophic level (producer).
Rabbit eats grass: second level.
Crow feeds on the herbivore-eaters: third level.
Vulture is at the fourth level.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2020-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Digestive enzymes",q:"The enzyme enterokinase helps in conversion of :",o:["trypsinogen into trypsin","caseinogen into pepsin","pepsinogen into pepsin","protein into polypeptides"],c:0,s:"Enterokinase of the intestinal juice activates trypsinogen into trypsin.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2020-q053",pn:53,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Digestion and Absorption",st:"Alimentary canal",q:"Identify the correct statement with reference to human digestive system.",o:["Serosa is the innermost layer of the alimentary canal.","Ileum is a highly coiled part.","Vermiform appendix arises from duodenum.","Ileum opens into small intestine"],c:1,s:`Ileum is the highly coiled last part of the small intestine.
Serosa is the outermost layer; the appendix arises from the caecum; the ileum opens into the large intestine.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Digestion and Absorption"},{id:"neet-2020-q054",pn:54,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Plant Growth and Development",st:"Gibberellins",q:"Name the plant growth regulator which upon spraying on sugarcane crop. Increases the length of stem. Thus increasing the yield of sugarcane crop.",o:["Gibberellin","Ethylene","Abscisic acid","Cytokinin"],c:0,s:"Gibberellins increase the stem length of sugarcane, so the yield rises.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Growth and Development"},{id:"neet-2020-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Restriction enzymes",q:"Identify the wrong statement with regard to Restriction Enzymes.",o:["They cut the strand of DNA at palindromic sites.","They are useful in genetic engineering.","Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.","Each restriction enzyme functions by inspection the of a DNA sequence."],c:0,s:`Restriction enzymes inspect the DNA and cut at a specific recognition sequence, which is palindromic.
Sticky ends made by them can be joined by DNA ligase.
The printed key (1) is kept.
Note: Printed answer 1 kept. Statement (4) is garbled in the printed paper, and statement (1) is true for restriction enzymes (they cut at palindromic sites), so the printed key may be wrong.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q056",pn:56,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Biomolecules and functions",q:`Match the following :
(a) Inhibitor of catalytic activity  (i) Ricin
(b) Possess peptide bonds  (ii) Malonate
(c) Cell wall material in fungi  (iii) Chitin
(d) Secondary metabolite  (iv) Collagen
Choose the correct option from the following :`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Malonate inhibits enzyme activity (a-ii).
Collagen is a protein with peptide bonds (b-iv).
Chitin is fungal cell wall material (c-iii).
Ricin is a secondary metabolite (d-i).`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2020-q057",pn:57,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structural Organisation in Animals",st:"Epithelial tissue",q:"Goblet cells of alimentary canal are modified from :",o:["Columnar epithelial cells","Chondrocytes","Compound epithelial cells","Squamous epithelial cells"],c:0,s:"Goblet cells that secrete mucus are modified columnar epithelial cells.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Structural Organisation in Animals"},{id:"neet-2020-q058",pn:58,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Vertebrate classes",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option.
(a) 6-15 pairs of gill slits  (i) Trygon
(b) Heterocercal  (ii) Cyclostomes
(c) Air Bladder  (iii) Chondrichthyes
(d) Poison sting  (iv) Osteichthyes`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Cyclostomes have 6-15 pairs of gill slits.
Chondrichthyes have a heterocercal tail.
Osteichthyes have an air bladder.
Trygon has a poison sting.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q059",pn:59,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I",q:"Dissolution of the synaptonemal complex occurs during :",o:["Zygotene","Diplotene","Leptotene","Pachytene"],c:1,s:"The synaptonemal complex forms in zygotene and dissolves in diplotene.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2020-q060",pn:60,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"Transcription",q:"Name the enzyme that facilitates opening of DNA helix during transcription.",o:["DNA helicase","DNA polymerase","RNA polymerase","DNA ligase"],c:2,s:"RNA polymerase opens the DNA helix and makes RNA during transcription.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2020-q061",pn:61,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance",st:"DNA structure",q:"Which of the following statements is correct",o:["Adenine pairs with thymine through one H-bond","Adenine pairs with thymine through three H-bonds","Adenine does not pair with thymine","Adenine pairs with thymine through two H-bonds"],c:3,s:`Adenine pairs with thymine by two hydrogen bonds.
Guanine pairs with cytosine by three.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Molecular Basis of Inheritance"},{id:"neet-2020-q062",pn:62,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Patterns of diversity",q:"Which of the following regions of the globe exhibits highest species diversity?",o:["Madagascar","Himalayas","Amazon foresta","Western Ghats of India"],c:2,s:"The Amazon rainforest has the highest species diversity on the earth.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2020-q063",pn:63,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Endocrine disorders",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option
(a) Pituitary gland  (i) Grave's disease
(b) Thyroid gland  (ii) Diabetes mellitus
(c) Adrenal gland  (iii) Diabetes insipidus
(d) Pancreas  (iv) Addison's disease`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:1,s:`Pituitary - diabetes insipidus (iii).
Thyroid - Grave's disease (i).
Adrenal - Addison's disease (iv).
Pancreas - diabetes mellitus (ii).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2020-q064",pn:64,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Nitrogen fixation",q:"The product(s) of reaction catalyzed by nitrogenase in root nodules of leguminous plants is/are :",o:["Nitrate alone","Ammonia and oxygen","Ammonia and hydrogen","Ammonia alone"],c:2,s:`Nitrogenase reduces N\u2082 to NH\u2083 and hydrogen is also released.
Printed key (3) is kept.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2020-q065",pn:65,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mineral Nutrition",st:"Functions of minerals",q:`Match the following concerning essential elements and their functions in plants :
(a) Iron  (i) Photolysis of Water
(b) Zinc  (ii) Pollen germination
(c) Boron  (iii) Required for chlorophyll biosynthesis
(d) Manganese  (iv) IAA biosynthesis
Select the correct option :`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],c:1,s:`Iron is needed for chlorophyll biosynthesis.
Zinc is needed for IAA biosynthesis.
Boron is needed for pollen germination.
Manganese is needed for photolysis of water.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Mineral Nutrition"},{id:"neet-2020-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Excretory Products and their Elimination",st:"Regulation of kidney function",q:"Which of the following would help in prevention of diuresis ?",o:["Reabsorption of Na\u207A and water form renal tubules due to aldosterone","Atrial natriuretic factor causes vasoconstriction","Decrease in secretion of renin by JG cells","More water reabsorption due to undersecretion of ADH"],c:0,s:`Aldosterone makes the tubules reabsorb Na\u207A and water, so less urine is passed.
This prevents diuresis.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Excretory Products and their Elimination"},{id:"neet-2020-q067",pn:67,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Fertilisation",q:"Meiotic division of the secondary oocyte is completed :",o:["At the time of copulation","After zygote formation","At the time of fusion of a sperm with an ovum","Prior to ovulation"],c:2,s:"The secondary oocyte finishes meiosis II only when a sperm enters it.",sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2020-q068",pn:68,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Animal Kingdom",st:"Animal examples",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option :
(a) Gregarious, polyphagous pest  (i) Asterias
(b) Adult with radial symmetry  (ii) Scorpion
(c) Book lung  (iii) Ctenoplana
(d) Bioluminescence  (iv) Locusta`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`Locusta is a gregarious polyphagous pest.
Asterias adult has radial symmetry.
Scorpion has book lungs.
Ctenoplana shows bioluminescence.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Animal Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q069",pn:69,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Locomotion and Movement",st:"Skeleton",q:`Match the following column and select the correct option.
(a) Floating Ribs  (i) Located between second and seventh ribs
(b) Acromion  (ii) Head of the Humerus
(c) Scapula  (iii) Clavicle
(d) Glenoid cavity  (iv) Do not connect with the sternum`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],c:2,s:`Floating ribs do not connect with the sternum.
Acromion is part of the clavicle region (clavicle).
Scapula is located between the second and seventh ribs.
Glenoid cavity holds the head of the humerus.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Locomotion and Movement"},{id:"neet-2020-q070",pn:70,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Secondary metabolites",q:"Secondary metabolites such as nicotine, strychnine and caffeine are produced by plants for their",o:["Growth response","Defence action","Effect on reproduction","Nutritive value"],c:1,s:"Nicotine, strychnine and caffeine protect the plant from herbivores: a defence action.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2020-q071",pn:71,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Applications of biotechnology",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option.
(a) Bt cotton  (i) Gene therapy
(b) Adenosine deaminase deficiency  (ii) Cellular defence
(c) RNAi  (iii) Detection of HIV infection
(d) PCR  (iv) Bacillus thuringiensis`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],c:3,s:`Bt cotton - Bacillus thuringiensis.
ADA deficiency - gene therapy.
RNAi - cellular defence.
PCR - detection of HIV infection.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2020-q072",pn:72,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Evolution",st:"Origin of life",q:"From his experiments, S.L. Miller produced amino acids by mixing the following in a closed flask :",o:["CH\u2083, H\u2082, NH\u2084 and water vapor at 800\xB0C","CH\u2084, H\u2082, NH\u2083 and water vapor at 600\xB0C","CH\u2083, H\u2082, NH\u2083 and water vapor at 600\xB0C","CH\u2084, H\u2082, NH\u2083 and water vapor at 800\xB0C"],c:3,s:`Miller used CH\u2084, H\u2082, NH\u2083 and water vapour at 800\xB0C with electric discharge in a closed flask.
This gave amino acids.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Evolution"},{id:"neet-2020-q073",pn:73,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Tools of biotechnology",q:`Match the organism with its use in biotechnology
(a) Bacillus thuringiensis  (i) Cloning vector
(b) Thermus aquaticus  (ii) Construction of first DNA molecule
(c) Agrobacterium tumefaciens  (iii) DNA polymerase
(d) Salmonella typhimurium  (iv) Cry proteins
Select the correct option from the following :`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],c:0,s:`Bacillus thuringiensis gives Cry proteins.
Thermus aquaticus gives Taq DNA polymerase.
Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a cloning vector.
Salmonella typhimurium was used in the construction of the first recombinant DNA molecule.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q074",pn:74,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology and its Applications",st:"Bt crops",q:"Bt cotton variety that was developed by the introduction of toxin gene of Bacillus thuringiensis (Bt) is resistant to :",o:["Fungal diseases","plant nematodes","Insect predators","Insect pests"],c:3,s:"Bt toxin kills insect pests such as bollworms, so Bt cotton resists insect pests.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology and its Applications"},{id:"neet-2020-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes",st:"Enzymes for DNA work",q:"Choose the correct pair from the following :",o:["Polymerases \u2013 Break the DNA into fragments","Nucleases \u2013 Separate the two strands of DNA","Exonucleases \u2013 Make cuts at specific positions within DNA","Ligases \u2013 Join the two DNA molecules"],c:3,s:`Ligases join two DNA molecules.
Nucleases cut DNA, exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends, and polymerases make DNA.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biotechnology: Principles and Processes"},{id:"neet-2020-q076",pn:76,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Ovule",q:"The body of the ovule is fused within the funicle",o:["Micropyle","Nucellus","Chalaza","Hilum"],c:3,s:"The hilum is the junction where the ovule body is fused with the funicle.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q077",pn:77,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Plant Kingdom",st:"Pteridophytes",q:"Strobili or cones are found in :",o:["Pteris","Marchantia","Equisetum","Salvinia"],c:2,s:"Equisetum bears strobili (cones).",sub:"Botany",ch:"Plant Kingdom"},{id:"neet-2020-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood cells",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option.
(a) Eosinophils  (i) Immune response
(b) Basophils  (ii) Phagocytosis
(c) Neutrophils  (iii) Release histaminase, destructive enzymes
(d) Lymphocytes  (iv) Release granules containing histamine`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Eosinophils release histaminase and destructive enzymes (iii).
Basophils release granules with histamine (iv).
Neutrophils do phagocytosis (ii).
Lymphocytes give immune response (i).`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Body Fluids and Circulation"},{id:"neet-2020-q079",pn:79,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Chemical bonds",q:"Identify the substances having glycosidic bond and peptide bond, respectively in their structure :",o:["Glycerol, trypsin","Cellulose, lecithin","Inulin, insulin","Chitin, cholesterol"],c:2,s:`Inulin is a polysaccharide with glycosidic bonds.
Insulin is a protein with peptide bonds.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2020-q080",pn:80,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ecosystem",st:"Productivity",q:"In relation to Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity of an ecosystem, which one of the following statements is correct ?",o:["Gross primary productivity is always more than net primary productivity.","Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity are one and same.","There is no relationship between Gross primary productivity and Net primary productivity.","Gross primary productivity is always less than net primary productivity."],c:0,s:`NPP = GPP - respiratory losses.
So GPP is always more than NPP.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Ecosystem"},{id:"neet-2020-q081",pn:81,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Human Reproduction",st:"Reproductive structures and hormones",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option -
(a) Placenta  (i) Androgens
(b) Zona pellucida  (ii) Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (hCG)
(c) Bulbo-urethral glands  (iii) Layer of the ovum
(d) Leydig cells  (iv) Lubrication of the penis`,o:["(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:2,s:`Placenta makes hCG.
Zona pellucida is a layer of the ovum.
Bulbo-urethral glands lubricate the penis.
Leydig cells make androgens.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Human Reproduction"},{id:"neet-2020-q082",pn:82,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Organisms and Populations",st:"Population attributes",q:"Which of the following is NOT an attribute of a population ?",o:["Natality","Mortality","Species interaction","Sex ratio"],c:2,s:`Birth rate, death rate and sex ratio are population attributes.
Species interaction belongs to a community.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Organisms and Populations"},{id:"neet-2020-q083",pn:83,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Neural Control and Coordination",st:"Ear",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option
(a) Organ of Corti  (i) Connects middle ear and pharynx
(b) Cochlea  (ii) Coiled part of the labyrinth
(c) Eustachian tube  (iii) Attached to the oval window
(d) Stapes  (iv) Located on the basilar membrane`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],c:1,s:`Organ of Corti is on the basilar membrane.
Cochlea is the coiled part of the labyrinth.
Eustachian tube connects the middle ear and pharynx.
Stapes is attached to the oval window.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Neural Control and Coordination"},{id:"neet-2020-q084",pn:84,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Biology)",st:"Proteins",q:"Which one of the following is the most abundant protein in the animals ?",o:["Collagen","Lectin","Insulin","Haemoglobin"],c:0,s:"Collagen is the most abundant protein in animals.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biomolecules (Biology)"},{id:"neet-2020-q085",pn:85,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Meiosis prophase I",q:`Match the following with respect to meiosis :
(a) Zygotene  (i) Terminalization
(b) Pachytene  (ii) Chiasmata
(c) Diplotene  (iii) Crossing Over
(d) Diakinesis  (iv) Synapsis
Select the Correct option from the following :`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],c:0,s:`Zygotene - synapsis.
Pachytene - crossing over.
Diplotene - chiasmata.
Diakinesis - terminalisation.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2020-q086",pn:86,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biodiversity and Conservation",st:"Species diversity",q:"According to Robert May, the global species diversity is about :",o:["20 million","50 million","7 million","1.5 million"],c:2,s:"Robert May estimated global species diversity at about 7 million.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Biodiversity and Conservation"},{id:"neet-2020-q087",pn:87,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Morphology of Flowering Plants",st:"Ovary position",q:"The ovary is half inferior in :",o:["Mustard","Sunflower","Plum","Brinjal"],c:2,s:`Plum (rose family) has a perigynous flower with half inferior ovary.
Mustard and brinjal have superior ovary; sunflower has inferior ovary.`,sub:"Botany",ch:"Morphology of Flowering Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q088",pn:88,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Coordination and Integration",st:"Hormones of pancreas and adrenal",q:"Select the correct statement :",o:["Glucagon is associated with hypoglycemia","Insulin acts on pancreatic cells and adipocyte","Insulin is associated with hyperglycemia","Glucocorticoids stimulate gluconeogenesis"],c:3,s:`Glucocorticoids such as cortisol stimulate gluconeogenesis, so (4) is correct.
Glucagon causes hyperglycaemia and insulin causes hypoglycaemia, so (1) and (3) are reversed.
Insulin acts mainly on hepatocytes and adipocytes, so (2) is wrong.`,sub:"Zoology",ch:"Chemical Coordination and Integration"},{id:"neet-2020-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Transport in Plants",st:"Guttation",q:"The process responsible for facilitating loss of water in liquid form the tip of grass blades at night and in early morning is",o:["Root pressure","Imbibition","Plasmolysis","Transpiration"],c:0,s:"Loss of water in liquid form from leaf tips of grass at night is guttation, caused by root pressure.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Transport in Plants"},{id:"neet-2020-q090",pn:90,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division",st:"Interphase",q:"Some dividing cells exit cell cycle and enter vegetative inactive stage. This is called quiescent stage (G0). This process occurs at the end of :",o:["G1 phase","S phase","G2 phase","M phase"],c:0,s:"Cells leave the cycle at the end of G\u2081 and enter the quiescent G\u2080 stage.",sub:"Botany",ch:"Cell Cycle and Cell Division"},{id:"neet-2020-q091",pn:91,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Oscillations",st:"Phase in SHM",q:"The phase difference between displacement and acceleration of a particle in a simple harmonic motion is :",o:["3\u03C0/2 rad","\u03C0/2 rad","Zero","\u03C0 rad"],c:3,s:`Let x = A sin(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
Velocity v = A\u03C9 cos(\u03C9t + \u03C6).
Acceleration a = \u2212\u03C9\xB2A sin(\u03C9t + \u03C6) = \u03C9\xB2A sin(\u03C9t + \u03C6 + \u03C0).
So acceleration leads displacement by \u03C0 rad.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Oscillations"},{id:"neet-2020-q092",pn:92,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Moving Charges and Magnetism",st:"Solenoid",q:"A long solenoid of 50 cm length having 100 turns carries a current of 2.5 A. The magnetic field at the centre of the solenoid is : (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m A\u207B\xB9)",o:["3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T"],c:3,s:`B = \u03BC\u2080nI = \u03BC\u2080(N/\u2113)I.
N/\u2113 = 100/0.5 = 200 turns per m.
B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 200 \xD7 2.5 = 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Moving Charges and Magnetism"},{id:"neet-2020-q093",pn:93,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Laws of Motion",st:"Atwood machine",q:"Two bodies of mass 4 kg and 6 kg are tied to the ends of a massless string. The string passes over a pulley which is frictionless (see figure). The acceleration of the system in terms of acceleration due to gravity (g) is:",o:["g/2","g/5","g/10","g"],c:1,s:`The heavier mass (6 kg) moves down.
a = (m\u2082 \u2212 m\u2081)g/(m\u2081 + m\u2082).
= (6 \u2212 4)g/(6 + 4) = 2g/10 = g/5.`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Frictionless pulley with a string; 4 kg block hangs on the left, 6 kg block hangs lower on the right",d:[["c",130,36,22],["c",130,36,1.5,"F"],["l",108,36,108,100],["l",152,36,152,126],["r",98,100,20,22],["r",142,126,20,22],["t",56,116,"4 kg"],["t",172,146,"6 kg"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Laws of Motion"},{id:"neet-2020-q094",pn:94,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Energy in EM waves",q:"The ratio of contributions made by the electric field and magnetic field components to the intensity of an electromagnetic wave is : (c = speed of electromagnetic waves)",o:["1 : 1","1 : c","1 : c\xB2","c : 1"],c:0,s:`The electric and magnetic fields carry equal energy densities in an EM wave.
So their contributions are in the ratio 1 : 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2020-q095",pn:95,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Potential and field",q:"In a certain region of space with volume 0.2 m\xB3, the electric potential is found to be 5 V throughout. The magnitude of electric field in this region is :",o:["0.5 N/C","1 N/C","5 N/C","zero"],c:3,s:`E = \u2212dV/dr.
The potential is constant (5 V) in the region, so the gradient is zero.
So E = 0.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2020-q096",pn:96,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Degrees of freedom",q:"The average thermal energy for a mono-atomic gas is : (k_B is Boltzmann constant and T absolute temperature)",o:["(3)/(2) k_BT","(5)/(2) k_BT","(7)/(2) k_BT","(1)/(2) k_BT"],c:0,s:`A monoatomic gas has 3 translational degrees of freedom.
Each gives \xBDk_BT.
Average energy = (3/2)k_BT.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2020-q097",pn:97,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Torque",q:"Find the torque about the origin when a force of 3\u0135 N acts on a particle whose position vector is 2k\u0302 m.",o:["6\u0135 N m","\u22126\xEE N m","6k\u0302 N m","6\xEE N m"],c:1,s:`\u03C4 = r \xD7 F = 2k\u0302 \xD7 3\u0135 = 6(k\u0302 \xD7 \u0135).
k\u0302 \xD7 \u0135 = \u2212\xEE.
So \u03C4 = \u22126\xEE N m.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2020-q098",pn:98,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Mean free path",q:"The mean free path for a gas, with molecular diameter d and number density n can be expressed as :",o:["1/(\u221A2 n\u03C0d\xB2)","1/(\u221A2 n\xB2\u03C0d\xB2)","1/(\u221A2 n\xB2\u03C0\xB2d\xB2)","1/(\u221A2 n\u03C0d)"],c:0,s:"Mean free path \u03BB = 1/(\u221A2 n\u03C0d\xB2).",sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2020-q099",pn:99,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Mass-energy relation",q:"The energy equivalent of 0.5 g of a substance is :",o:["4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","1.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","0.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J","4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J"],c:0,s:`E = mc\xB2.
m = 0.5 g = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg, c\xB2 = 9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 m\xB2/s\xB2.
E = 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 = 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J.
Note: Options (1) and (4) print the same value 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J; the printed solution has a typing error (10\u207B\xB3) but its working gives 4.5 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 J. Printed key 1 kept.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2020-q100",pn:100,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Screw gauge",q:"A screw gauge has least count of 0.01 mm and there are 50 divisions in its circular scale. The pitch of the screw gauge is :",o:["0.25 mm","0.5 mm","1.0 mm","0.01 mm"],c:1,s:`Least count = pitch / number of circular divisions.
0.01 mm = pitch/50.
Pitch = 0.01 \xD7 50 = 0.5 mm.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2020-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics",st:"Free expansion",q:"Two cylinders A and B of equal capacity are connected to each other via a stop cock. A contains an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure. B is completely evacuated. The entire system is thermally insulated. The stop cock is suddenly opened. The process is :",o:["adiabatic","isochoric","isobaric","isothermal"],c:3,s:`The gas expands into vacuum, so it does no work (W = 0).
The system is insulated, so no heat flows (Q = 0).
So \u0394U = 0 and, for an ideal gas, \u0394T = 0.
The temperature stays the same, so the printed key (isothermal) is taken.
Note: Printed key 4 (isothermal) kept; Q = 0 also makes the process adiabatic, but the temperature does not change.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermodynamics"},{id:"neet-2020-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Kinetic Theory",st:"Ideal gas equation",q:"A cylinder contains hydrogen gas at pressure of 249 kPa and temperature 27\xB0C. Its density is : (R = 8.3 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["0.2 kg/m\xB3","0.1 kg/m\xB3","0.02 kg/m\xB3","0.5 kg/m\xB3"],c:0,s:`PV = (m/M)RT, so density \u03C1 = PM/(RT).
P = 249 \xD7 10\xB3 Pa, M = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 kg/mol, T = 300 K.
\u03C1 = (249 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)/(8.3 \xD7 300) = 498/2490 = 0.2 kg/m\xB3.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Kinetic Theory"},{id:"neet-2020-q103",pn:103,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Nuclei",st:"Nuclear fission",q:"When a uranium isotope \xB2\xB3\u2075\u2089\u2082U is bombarded with a neutron, it generates \u2078\u2079\u2083\u2086Kr, three neutrons and :",o:["\u2079\xB9\u2084\u2080Zr","\xB9\u2070\xB9\u2083\u2086Kr","\xB9\u2070\xB3\u2083\u2086Kr","\xB9\u2074\u2074\u2085\u2086Ba"],c:3,s:`Reaction: \xB2\xB3\u2075\u2089\u2082U + \xB9\u2080n \u2192 \u2078\u2079\u2083\u2086Kr + X + 3 \xB9\u2080n.
Mass number: 235 + 1 = 89 + A + 3, so A = 144.
Charge: 92 = 36 + Z, so Z = 56.
X is \xB9\u2074\u2074\u2085\u2086Ba.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Nuclei"},{id:"neet-2020-q104",pn:104,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Drift velocity and mobility",q:"A charged particle having drift velocity of 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m s\u207B\xB9 in an electric field of 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 V m\u207B\xB9, has a mobility in m\xB2 V\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9 of :",o:["2.5 \xD7 10\u2076","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076","2.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075","2.25 \xD7 10\xB9\u2075"],c:0,s:`Mobility \u03BC = v_d/E.
= (7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070) = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2076 m\xB2 V\u207B\xB9 s\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2020-q105",pn:105,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Significant figures",q:"Taking into account of the significant figures, what is the value of 9.99 m \u2013 0.0099 m ?",o:["9.98 m","9.980 m","9.9 m","9.9801 m"],c:0,s:`9.99 \u2212 0.0099 = 9.9801.
In subtraction the result keeps as many decimal places as the term with fewer decimal places.
9.99 has 2 decimal places, so the answer is 9.98 m.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2020-q106",pn:106,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Magnetism and Matter",st:"Susceptibility and permeability",q:"An iron rod of susceptibility 599 is subjected to a magnetising field of 1200 A m\u207B\xB9. The permeability of the material of the rod is : (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m A\u207B\xB9)",o:["8.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T m A\u207B\xB9","2.4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T m A\u207B\xB9","2.4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m A\u207B\xB9","2.4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T m A\u207B\xB9"],c:3,s:`Relative permeability \u03BC\u1D63 = 1 + \u03C7 = 1 + 599 = 600.
\u03BC = \u03BC\u2080\u03BC\u1D63 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 600.
= 2.4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T m A\u207B\xB9.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Magnetism and Matter"},{id:"neet-2020-q107",pn:107,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electric Charges and Fields",st:"Field of a charged sphere",q:"A spherical conductor of radius 10 cm has a charge of 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 C distributed uniformly. What is the magnitude of electric field at a point 15 cm from the centre of the sphere ? (1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2/C\xB2)",o:["1.28 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C","1.28 \xD7 10\u2076 N/C","1.28 \xD7 10\u2077 N/C","1.28 \xD7 10\u2074 N/C"],c:0,s:`The point is outside the sphere, so it acts like a point charge at the centre.
E = kQ/r\xB2 = (9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077)/(0.15)\xB2.
= 2880/0.0225 = 1.28 \xD7 10\u2075 N/C.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electric Charges and Fields"},{id:"neet-2020-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"LCR circuit power factor",q:"A series LCR circuit is connected to an voltage source. When L is removed from the circuit, the phase difference between current and voltage is \u03C0/3. If instead C is removed from the circuit, the phase difference is again \u03C0/3 between current and voltage. The power factor of the circuit is :",o:["0.5","1.0","\u20131.0","zero"],c:1,s:`Without L: tan \u03C6 = X_C/R. Without C: tan \u03C6 = X_L/R.
The phase differences are equal (\u03C0/3), so X_C = X_L.
So the circuit is in resonance and Z = R.
Power factor = cos \u03C6 = R/Z = 1.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2020-q109",pn:109,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids",st:"Capillary rise",q:"A capillary tube of radius r is immersed in water and water rises in it to a height h. The mass of the water in the capillary is 5g. Another capillary tube of radius 2r is immersed in water. The mass of water that will rise in this tube is :",o:["5.0 g","10.0 g","20.0 g","2.5 g"],c:1,s:`Height h = 2T cos\u03B8/(r\u03C1g), so h \u221D 1/r.
Mass m = \u03C0r\xB2h\u03C1 \u221D r\xB2 \xD7 (1/r) = r.
m\u2082/m\u2081 = r\u2082/r\u2081 = 2r/r = 2.
m\u2082 = 2 \xD7 5 = 10 g.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Fluids"},{id:"neet-2020-q110",pn:110,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Fringe width",q:"In Young's double slit experiment, if the separation between coherent sources is halved and the distance of the screen from the coherent sources is doubled, then the fringe width becomes :",o:["half","four times","one-fourth","double"],c:1,s:`Fringe width \u03B2 = \u03BBD/d.
New D = 2D and new d = d/2.
\u03B2' = \u03BB(2D)/(d/2) = 4\u03BBD/d = 4\u03B2.
The fringe width becomes four times.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2020-q111",pn:111,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Logic gates",q:"For the logic circuit shown, the truth table is :",o:["A B Y: 00\u21920, 01\u21921, 10\u21921, 11\u21921","A B Y: 00\u21921, 01\u21921, 10\u21921, 11\u21920","A B Y: 00\u21921, 01\u21920, 10\u21920, 11\u21920","A B Y: 00\u21920, 01\u21920, 10\u21920, 11\u21921"],c:3,s:`Each input first goes through a NOT gate, so the OR-type gate with a bubble (NOR) gets A\u0305 and B\u0305.
Y = NOR(A\u0305, B\u0305) = (A\u0305 + B\u0305)\u203E = A\xB7B.
This is an AND gate: Y = 1 only when A = B = 1.`,fig:{w:280,h:90,alt:"Logic circuit: A and B each pass through a NOT gate and feed a NOR gate with output Y",d:[["t",0,19,"A"],["t",0,69,"B"],["l",14,15,50,15],["p","M50 6L50 24L68 15Z"],["c",71,15,3],["l",74,15,140,15],["l",140,15,140,28],["l",140,28,176,28],["l",14,65,50,65],["p","M50 56L50 74L68 65Z"],["c",71,65,3],["l",74,65,140,65],["l",140,65,140,50],["l",140,50,176,50],["p","M172 20C186 20 202 26 214 39C202 52 186 58 172 58C180 48 180 30 172 20Z"],["c",217,39,3],["l",220,39,255,39],["t",260,43,"Y"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2020-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Resistor colour code",q:"The color code of a resistance is given below : Yellow, Violet, Brown, Gold. The values of resistance and tolerance, respectively, are :",o:["4.7 k\u03A9, 10%","4.7 k\u03A9, 5%","470 \u03A9, 5%","470 k\u03A9, 5%"],c:2,s:`Yellow = 4 and violet = 7 give the digits 47.
Brown = multiplier 10\xB9.
R = 47 \xD7 10\xB9 = 470 \u03A9.
Gold = tolerance \xB15%.
Note: In the page image the options (3) and (4) look alike (both read as 470 k\u03A9, 5%); written here as (3) 470 \u03A9, 5% to match the printed key 3 and working.`,fig:{w:370,h:95,alt:"Resistor with four colour bands labelled Yellow, Violet, Brown, Gold",d:[["l",8,32,48,32],["r",48,5,267,55],["l",315,32,355,32],["r",95,5,7,50,"F"],["r",153,5,7,50,"F"],["r",211,5,7,50,"F"],["r",267,5,7,50,"F"],["t",98,76,"Yellow","m"],["t",156,76,"Violet","m"],["t",214,76,"Brown","m"],["t",270,76,"Gold","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2020-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Dielectric",q:"The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor with air as medium is 6 \u03BCF. With the introduction of a dielectric medium, the capacitance becomes 30 \u03BCF. The permittivity of the medium is : (\u03B5\u2080 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2)",o:["1.77 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2","0.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2","5.00 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2","0.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2"],c:1,s:`Dielectric constant \u03B5\u1D63 = C/C\u2080 = 30/6 = 5.
Permittivity \u03B5 = \u03B5\u2080\u03B5\u1D63 = 8.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 \xD7 5.
= 4.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB9 = 0.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 C\xB2 N\u207B\xB9 m\u207B\xB2.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2020-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Equations of motion",q:"A ball is thrown vertically downward with a velocity of 20 m/s from the top of a tower. It hits the ground after some time with a velocity of 80 m/s. The height of the tower is : (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["340 m","320 m","300 m","360 m"],c:2,s:`Use v\xB2 = u\xB2 + 2gh.
80\xB2 = 20\xB2 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 h.
6400 = 400 + 20h.
h = 6000/20 = 300 m.`,sfig:{w:160,h:130,alt:"Ball thrown down from tower of height h: 20 m/s at the top, 80 m/s at the ground",d:[["l",10,15,22,15],["l",16,15,16,50],["t",12,66,"h"],["l",16,76,16,110],["l",10,110,22,110],["l",10,110,115,110],["c",60,15,3,"F"],["l",60,20,60,108,"da"],["t",72,19,"20 m/s"],["t",68,100,"80 m/s"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Motion in a Straight Line"},{id:"neet-2020-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Gravitation",st:"Variation of g with height",q:"A body weighs 72 N on the surface of the earth. What is the gravitational force on it, at a height equal to half the radius of the earth ?",o:["32 N","30 N","24 N","48 N"],c:0,s:`Weight at height h = W/(1 + h/R)\xB2.
h = R/2, so (1 + 1/2)\xB2 = 9/4.
W_h = 72/(9/4) = 72 \xD7 4/9 = 32 N.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Gravitation"},{id:"neet-2020-q116",pn:116,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion",st:"Centre of mass",q:"Two particles of mass 5 kg and 10 kg respectively are attached to the two ends of a rigid rod of length 1 m with negligible mass. The centre of mass of the system from the 5 kg particle is nearly at a distance of",o:["50 cm","67 cm","80 cm","33 cm"],c:1,s:`Take the 5 kg particle at x = 0 and the 10 kg particle at x = 1 m.
x_cm = (5 \xD7 0 + 10 \xD7 1)/(5 + 10) = 10/15 = 2/3 m.
\u2248 67 cm from the 5 kg particle.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"System of Particles and Rotational Motion"},{id:"neet-2020-q117",pn:117,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"p-n junction",q:"The increase in the width of the depletion region in a p-n junction diode is due to",o:["reverse bias only","both forward bias and reverse bias","increase in forward current","forward bias only"],c:0,s:`In reverse bias the battery pulls the majority carriers away from the junction.
So more immobile ions are uncovered and the depletion region widens.
In forward bias it narrows.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2020-q118",pn:118,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"Photoelectric effect",q:"Light of frequency 1.5 times the threshold frequency is incident on a photosensitive material. What will be the photoelectric current if the frequency is halved and intensity is doubled ?",o:["four times","one-forth","zero","doubled"],c:2,s:`New frequency = 1.5\u03BD\u2080/2 = 0.75\u03BD\u2080.
This is less than the threshold frequency \u03BD\u2080.
So no electron is emitted, whatever the intensity, and the photoelectric current is zero.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2020-q119",pn:119,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Resolving power",q:"Assume that light of wavelength 600 nm is coming from a star. The limit of resolution of telescope whose objective has a diameter of 2 m is",o:["1.83 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 rad","7.32 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 rad","6.00 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 rad","3.66 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 rad"],c:3,s:`Limit of resolution \u0394\u03B8 = 1.22\u03BB/a.
= (1.22 \xD7 600 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079)/2.
= 3.66 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 rad.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2020-q120",pn:120,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Metre bridge",q:"A resistance wire connected in the left gap of a metre bridge balances a 10 \u03A9 resistance in the right gap at a point which divides the bridge wire in the ratio 3 : 2. If the length of the resistance wire is 1.5 m, then the length of 1 \u03A9 of the resistance wire is :",o:["1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m","1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m"],c:0,s:`At balance R/10 = \u2113\u2081/\u2113\u2082 = 3/2.
R = 15 \u03A9.
15 \u03A9 of wire has length 1.5 m.
So 1 \u03A9 has length 1.5/15 = 0.1 m = 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 m.`,sfig:{w:190,h:140,alt:"Metre bridge: R in the left gap and 10 ohm in the right gap, bridge wire with jockey dividing it into l1 and l2, battery across the wire",d:[["p","M8 121V25H25L29 25L32 19L37 31L42 19L47 31L52 19L56 25H118L122 25L125 19L130 31L135 19L140 31L145 19L149 25H176V121H45"],["l",8,121,36,121],["l",36,115,36,127,"B"],["l",45,111,45,131],["l",8,76,176,76],["p","M90 25V50H76V72","a"],["t",42,12,"R","m"],["t",136,12,"10 \u03A9","m"],["t",40,68,"\u2113\u2081","m"],["t",134,68,"\u2113\u2082","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2020-q121",pn:121,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Energy in EM waves",q:"Light with an average flux of 20 W/cm\xB2 falls on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence having surface area 20 cm\xB2. The energy received by the surface during time span of 1 minute is :",o:["12 \xD7 10\xB3 J","24 \xD7 10\xB3 J","48 \xD7 10\xB3 J","10 \xD7 10\xB3 J"],c:1,s:`Power = flux \xD7 area = 20 W/cm\xB2 \xD7 20 cm\xB2 = 400 W.
Energy = power \xD7 time = 400 \xD7 60 = 24000 J.
= 24 \xD7 10\xB3 J.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electromagnetic Waves"},{id:"neet-2020-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments",st:"Prism",q:"A ray is incident at an angle of incidence i on one surface of a small angle prism (with angle of prism A) and emerges normally from the opposite surface. If the refractive index of the material of the prism is \u03BC, then the angle of incidence is nearly equal to",o:["2A/\u03BC","\u03BCA","\u03BCA/2","A/2\u03BC"],c:1,s:`The ray leaves normally, so r\u2082 = 0.
r\u2081 + r\u2082 = A, so r\u2081 = A.
Snell's law: sin i = \u03BC sin r\u2081.
For small angles i = \u03BC r\u2081 = \u03BCA.`,sfig:{w:150,h:140,alt:"Ray enters the left face of a prism of apex angle A at angle of incidence i, refracts at r1 and leaves the right face along the normal",d:[["p","M66 15L28 120H102Z"],["l",5,88,26,75.8,"a"],["l",26,75.8,48,63],["l",48,63,110,42],["l",20,53,72,72,"d"],["p","M36 70A14 14 0 0 1 35 58"],["t",19,70,"i","e"],["t",75,80,"r\u2081"],["t",66,38,"A","m"],["t",65,113,"\u03BC","m"]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments"},{id:"neet-2020-q123",pn:123,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alternating Current",st:"Capacitor in AC circuit",q:"A 40 \u03BCF capacitor is connected to a 200 V, 50 Hz ac supply. The rms value of the current in the circuit is, nearly :",o:["2.05 A","2.5 A","25.1 A","1.7 A"],c:1,s:`Capacitive reactance X_C = 1/(\u03C9C), so I = V/X_C = V\u03C9C.
\u03C9 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 50 = 314 rad/s.
I = 200 \xD7 314 \xD7 40 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 2.5 A.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Alternating Current"},{id:"neet-2020-q124",pn:124,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Dimensions",q:"Dimensions of stress are :",o:["[ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]","[ML\u2070T\u207B\xB2]","[ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2]","[MLT\u207B\xB2]"],c:2,s:`Stress = force/area.
Force = [MLT\u207B\xB2], area = [L\xB2].
Stress = [MLT\u207B\xB2]/[L\xB2] = [ML\u207B\xB9T\u207B\xB2].`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2020-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Wave Optics",st:"Brewster angle",q:"The Brewster's angle i_b for an interface should be :",o:["30\xB0 < i_b < 45\xB0","45\xB0 < i_b < 90\xB0","i_b = 90\xB0","0\xB0 < i_b < 30\xB0"],c:1,s:`tan i_b = \u03BC\u2082/\u03BC\u2081 (relative refractive index).
For an interface with \u03BC\u2082 > \u03BC\u2081 the ratio is greater than 1.
So tan i_b > 1, which gives 45\xB0 < i_b < 90\xB0.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Wave Optics"},{id:"neet-2020-q126",pn:126,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Mechanical Properties of Solids",st:"Young's modulus",q:"A wire of length L, area of cross section A is hanging from a fixed support. The length of the wire changes to L\u2081 when mass M is suspended from its free end. The expression for Young's modulus is :",o:["Mg(L\u2081\u2013L)/(AL)","MgL/(AL\u2081)","MgL/(A(L\u2081\u2013L))","MgL\u2081/(AL)"],c:2,s:`Young's modulus Y = stress/strain = (F/A)/(\u0394L/L).
F = Mg and the extension \u0394L = L\u2081 \u2212 L.
Y = MgL/(A(L\u2081 \u2212 L)).`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Mechanical Properties of Solids"},{id:"neet-2020-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance",st:"Dipole potential",q:"A short electric dipole has a dipole moment of 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 C m. The electric potential due to the dipole at a point at a distance of 0.6 m from the center of the dipole, situated on a line making an angle of 60\xB0 with the dipole axis is : (1/(4\u03C0\u03B5\u2080) = 9 \xD7 10\u2079 N m\xB2/C\xB2)",o:["200 V","400 V","Zero","50 V"],c:0,s:`V = k p cos \u03B8/r\xB2.
= (9 \xD7 10\u2079 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 \xD7 cos 60\xB0)/(0.6)\xB2.
= (144 \xD7 0.5)/0.36 = 200 V.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance"},{id:"neet-2020-q128",pn:128,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Waves",st:"Beats",q:"In a guitar, two strings A and B made of same material are slightly out of tune and produce beats of frequency 6 Hz. When tension in B is slightly decreased, the beat frequency increases to 7 Hz. If the frequency of A is 530 Hz, the original frequency of B will be :",o:["524 Hz","536 Hz","537 Hz","523 Hz"],c:0,s:`B is 530 \xB1 6 = 536 Hz or 524 Hz.
Frequency \u221D \u221Atension, so lowering the tension lowers the frequency of B.
If B were 536 Hz, the beat frequency would fall below 6 Hz.
If B is 524 Hz, going lower makes the beats rise to 7 Hz.
So B was 524 Hz.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Waves"},{id:"neet-2020-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",st:"de Broglie wavelength",q:"An electron is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of V volt. If the de Broglie wavelength of the electron is 1.227 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 nm, the potential difference is :",o:["10\xB2 V","10\xB3 V","10\u2074 V","10 V"],c:2,s:`For an electron \u03BB = 12.27/\u221AV \xC5.
\u03BB = 1.227 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 nm = 0.1227 \xC5.
\u221AV = 12.27/0.1227 = 100.
V = 10\u2074 volt.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter"},{id:"neet-2020-q130",pn:130,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Resistance and temperature",q:"The solids which have the negative temperature coefficient of resistance are :",o:["insulators only","semiconductors only","insulators and semiconductors","metals"],c:2,s:`In insulators and semiconductors the resistance falls as temperature rises, because more charge carriers are freed.
Metals have a positive coefficient.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2020-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Units and Measurements",st:"Unit conversion",q:"The energy required to break one bond in DNA is 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 J. This value in eV is nearly :",o:["0.6","0.06","0.006","6"],c:1,s:`1 eV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J.
E = 10\u207B\xB2\u2070/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079) eV.
= 0.625 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 = 0.0625 eV \u2248 0.06 eV.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Units and Measurements"},{id:"neet-2020-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Heat capacity",q:"The quantities of heat required to raise the temperature of two solid copper spheres of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 (r\u2081 = 1.5 r\u2082) through 1 K are in the ratio :",o:["9/4","3/2","5/3","27/8"],c:3,s:`Heat Q = ms\u0394T, and s and \u0394T are the same.
So Q \u221D m = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1 \u221D r\xB3.
Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB3 = (1.5)\xB3 = 27/8.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Thermal Properties of Matter"},{id:"neet-2020-q133",pn:133,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Current Electricity",st:"Resistivity and temperature",q:"Which of the following graph represents the variation of resistivity (\u03C1) with temperature (T) for copper ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`For metals like copper, resistivity rises with temperature, nearly in proportion to T, with a non-linear part at very low temperature.
The curve that rises with T and flattens at low T is option (2).
Note: Option drawings were read from the page image; the key printed is 2 and the printed reason (nearly proportional to T with a non-linear region at low T) is also close to option (4).`,fig:{w:410,h:245,alt:"Four graphs of resistivity against temperature: (1) falling convex curve, (2) curve rising with increasing slope from a flat start, (3) falling curve bending steeply downward, (4) rising straight line",d:[["l",48,0,48,95],["l",48,95,170,95],["t",42,48,"\u03C1","e"],["t",0,66,"(1)"],["t",118,111,"T","m"],["p","M64 5C68 50 85 78 119 93"],["l",268,0,268,95],["l",268,95,390,95],["t",262,48,"\u03C1","e"],["t",220,66,"(2)"],["t",344,111,"T","m"],["p","M271 88C298 85 322 68 336 23"],["l",48,120,48,220],["l",48,220,170,220],["t",42,168,"\u03C1","e"],["t",0,188,"(3)"],["t",118,234,"T","m"],["p","M61 140C90 150 120 170 128 205"],["l",268,120,268,220],["l",268,220,390,220],["t",262,168,"\u03C1","e"],["t",220,188,"(4)"],["t",344,234,"T","m"],["l",271,192,359,147]]},sub:"Physics",ch:"Current Electricity"},{id:"neet-2020-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Semiconductor Electronics",st:"Transistor",q:"For transistor action, which of the following statements is correct",o:["Base, emitter and collector regions should have same size.","Both emitter junction as well as the collector junction are forward biased.","The base region must be very thin and lightly doped.","Base, emitter and collector regions should have same doping concentrations"],c:2,s:`In a transistor the base is made very thin and lightly doped so that most carriers from the emitter cross to the collector.
The three regions differ in size and doping, and the emitter junction is forward biased while the collector junction is reverse biased.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Semiconductor Electronics"},{id:"neet-2020-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Atoms",st:"Bohr model",q:"For which one of the following, Bohr model is not valid?",o:["Singly ionized helium atom (He\u207A)","Deuteron atom","Singly ionized neon atom (Ne\u207A)","Hydrogen atom"],c:2,s:`Bohr's model works only for one-electron species (H, D, He\u207A, Li\xB2\u207A).
Ne\u207A has 9 electrons, so the model is not valid for it.`,sub:"Physics",ch:"Atoms"},{id:"neet-2020-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation number",q:"What is the change in oxidation number of carbon in the following reaction ? CH\u2084(g) + 4Cl\u2082(g) \u2192 CCl\u2084(l) + 4HCl(g)",o:["0 to + 4","\u2013 4 to + 4","0 to \u2013 4","+ 4 to + 4"],c:1,s:`In CH\u2084: C + 4(+1) = 0, so C = \u22124.
In CCl\u2084: C + 4(\u22121) = 0, so C = +4.
The change is \u22124 to +4.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Redox Reactions"},{id:"neet-2020-q137",pn:137,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Electrolysis",q:"On electrolysis of dil. Sulphuric acid using Platinum (Pt) electrode, the product obtained at anode will be :",o:["Oxygen gas","H\u2082S gas","SO\u2082 gas","Hydrogen gas"],c:0,s:`In dilute H\u2082SO\u2084 water is oxidised more easily than sulphate at the Pt anode.
Anode: 2H\u2082O \u2192 O\u2082 + 4H\u207A + 4e\u207B.
So oxygen gas is given at the anode.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2020-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"Collision theory",q:"An increase in the concentration of the reactants of reaction leads to change in :",o:["Heat of reaction","Threshold energy","Collision frequency","Activation energy"],c:2,s:`More reactant concentration means more molecules per unit volume.
So the collision frequency rises.
Heat of reaction, threshold energy and activation energy stay unchanged.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2020-q139",pn:139,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Aldol condensation",q:"Reaction between benzaldehyde and acetophenone in presence of dilute NaOH is known as",o:["Cannizzaro's reaction","Cross Cannizzaro's reaction","Cross Aldol condensation","Aldol condensation"],c:2,s:`Acetophenone has \u03B1-hydrogens; benzaldehyde has none.
In dilute NaOH the acetophenone enolate attacks benzaldehyde and water is lost.
Two different carbonyl compounds react, so it is a cross aldol condensation.`,sfig:{w:400,h:130,alt:"Benzaldehyde plus acetophenone with dilute NaOH and heat give the cross-aldol product, benzylideneacetophenone (major)",d:[["p","M30.0 53.0L17.0 60.5L17.0 75.5L30.0 83.0L43.0 75.5L43.0 60.5Z"],["l",20.1,64.1,20.1,71.9],["l",31.6,78.5,38.3,74.6],["l",38.3,61.4,31.6,57.5],["l",30,53,30,40],["t",30,36,"CHO","m"],["t",58,72,"+","m"],["p","M92.0 53.0L79.0 60.5L79.0 75.5L92.0 83.0L105.0 75.5L105.0 60.5Z"],["l",82.1,64.1,82.1,71.9],["l",93.6,78.5,100.3,74.6],["l",100.3,61.4,93.6,57.5],["l",105,60.5,118,51.5],["l",126.2,40,126.2,26],["l",121.8,40,121.8,26],["t",124,20,"O","m"],["t",124,51,"C","m"],["l",129,50,138,58],["t",136,70,"CH\u2083"],["l",165,60,227,60,"a"],["t",196,53,"dil. NaOH","ms"],["t",196,73,"\u0394","ms"],["p","M268.0 53.0L255.0 60.5L255.0 75.5L268.0 83.0L281.0 75.5L281.0 60.5Z"],["l",258.1,64.1,258.1,71.9],["l",269.6,78.5,276.3,74.6],["l",276.3,61.4,269.6,57.5],["l",281,60.5,295,52],["l",294,54,308,61],["l",296,50,310,57],["t",311,64,"CH"],["l",332,56,344,45],["l",346.2,45,346.2,29],["l",341.8,45,341.8,29],["t",344,23,"O","m"],["l",344,45,354,64.5],["p","M367.0 57.0L354.0 64.5L354.0 79.5L367.0 87.0L380.0 79.5L380.0 64.5Z"],["l",357.1,68.1,357.1,75.9],["l",368.6,82.5,375.3,78.6],["l",375.3,65.4,368.6,61.5],["t",326,100,"(Major)","m"],["t",326,116,"Cross-Aldol product","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2020-q140",pn:140,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Wurtz reaction",q:"Which of the following alkane cannot be made in good yield by Wurtz reaction ?",o:["2,3-Dimethylbutane","n-Heptane","n-Butane","n-Hexane"],c:1,s:`Wurtz coupling of one alkyl halide gives a symmetrical alkane.
2,3-Dimethylbutane, n-butane and n-hexane are symmetrical.
n-Heptane would need two different halides, giving a mixture, so the yield is poor.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2020-q141",pn:141,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Polymers",st:"Natural and synthetic polymers",q:"Which of the following is a natural polymer ?",o:["Poly (Butadiene-styrene)","polybutadiene","Poly (Butadiene-acrylonitrile)","cis-1,4-polyisoprene"],c:3,s:`Natural rubber is cis-1,4-polyisoprene.
The other three are synthetic polymers.
Note: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Polymers"},{id:"neet-2020-q142",pn:142,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"States of Matter",st:"Dalton's law",q:"A mixture of N\u2082 and Ar gases in a cylinder contains 7 g of N\u2082 and 8 g of Ar. If the total pressure of the mixture of the gases in the cylinder is 27 bar, the partial pressure of N\u2082 is : [Use atomic masses (in g mol\u207B\xB9) : N = 14, Ar = 40]",o:["12 bar","15 bar","18 bar","9 bar"],c:1,s:`Moles of N\u2082 = 7/28 = 1/4.
Moles of Ar = 8/40 = 1/5.
Mole fraction of N\u2082 = (1/4)/(1/4 + 1/5) = (1/4)/(9/20) = 5/9.
p(N\u2082) = 27 \xD7 5/9 = 15 bar.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"States of Matter"},{id:"neet-2020-q143",pn:143,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Hydrogen",st:"Hydrogen compounds",q:`Match the following and identify the correct option.
(a) CO(g) + H\u2082(g)  (i) Mg(HCO\u2083)\u2082 + Ca(HCO\u2083)\u2082
(b) Temporary hardness of water  (ii) An electron deficient hydride
(c) B\u2082H\u2086  (iii) Synthesis gas
(d) H\u2082O\u2082  (iv) Non-planar structure`,o:["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],c:3,s:`CO + H\u2082 is synthesis gas (iii).
Temporary hardness is due to Mg(HCO\u2083)\u2082 and Ca(HCO\u2083)\u2082 (i).
B\u2082H\u2086 is electron deficient (ii).
H\u2082O\u2082 has a non-planar (open book) structure (iv).`,sfig:{w:180,h:135,alt:"H2O2 as an open book: two planes meeting at the O-O bond, one H on each plane pointing away from the other",d:[["l",8,15,117,7],["l",117,7,118,65],["l",8,15,15,75],["p","M15 75L65 123L165 110L118 65"],["l",15,75,48,70,"d"],["l",118,65,92,67,"d"],["l",59,69,79,68],["t",55,74,"O","m"],["t",85,72,"O","m"],["t",33,36,"H","m"],["p","M51 62L36 41L43 38Z","F"],["l",89,76,110,90],["t",118,102,"H","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrogen"},{id:"neet-2020-q144",pn:144,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Sign of \u0394H and \u0394S",q:"For the reaction, 2Cl(g) \u2192 Cl\u2082(g), the correct option is :",o:["\u0394_rH > 0 and \u0394_rS < 0","\u0394_rH < 0 and \u0394_rS > 0","\u0394_rH < 0 and \u0394_rS < 0","\u0394_rH > 0 and \u0394_rS > 0"],c:2,s:`A bond forms, so energy is released: \u0394H < 0.
Two moles of gas become one mole, so disorder falls: \u0394S < 0.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2020-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The Solid State",st:"bcc unit cell",q:"An element has a body centered cubic (bcc) structure with a cell edge of 288 pm. The atomic radius is :",o:["(\u221A2/4) \xD7 288 pm","(4/\u221A3) \xD7 288 pm","(4/\u221A2) \xD7 288 pm","(\u221A3/4) \xD7 288 pm"],c:3,s:`In bcc the atoms touch along the body diagonal, which is \u221A3a.
The diagonal carries 4r, so \u221A3a = 4r.
r = (\u221A3/4) \xD7 288 pm.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The Solid State"},{id:"neet-2020-q146",pn:146,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Complex formation",q:"Urea reacts with water to form A which will decompose to form B. B when passed through Cu\xB2\u207A(aq), deep blue colour solution C is formed. What is the formula of C from the following ?",o:["[Cu(NH\u2083)\u2084]\xB2\u207A","Cu(OH)\u2082","CuCO\u2083.Cu(OH)\u2082","CuSO\u2084"],c:0,s:`Urea + water gives ammonium carbonate-type product A (NH\u2084OH with CO\u2082).
A decomposes on heating to give ammonia, B = NH\u2083.
Cu\xB2\u207A + 4NH\u2083 \u2192 [Cu(NH\u2083)\u2084]\xB2\u207A, which is deep blue.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2020-q147",pn:147,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Grignard reaction",q:"Reaction between acetone and methylmagnesium chloride followed by hydrolysis will give :",o:["Sec. butyl alcohol","Tert. butyl alcohol","Isobutyl alcohol","Isopropyl alcohol"],c:1,s:`CH\u2083 of the Grignard reagent adds to the C=O carbon of acetone.
This gives (CH\u2083)\u2083C\u2013OMgCl.
Hydrolysis gives (CH\u2083)\u2083C\u2013OH, tert-butyl alcohol.`,sfig:{w:400,h:210,alt:"Acetone adds CH3MgCl at the C=O carbon to give (CH3)3C-O(-)MgCl(+), and H2O then gives tert-butyl alcohol (CH3)3C-OH",d:[["t",13,65,"CH\u2083"],["l",42,61,62,61],["t",70,65,"C","m"],["l",78,61,100,61],["t",104,65,"CH\u2083"],["l",68,67,68,84],["l",73,67,73,84],["t",71,98,"O","m"],["p","M182 42C175 8 95 0 72 44","a"],["p","M77 74C92 76 92 91 81 93","a"],["l",143,60,222,60,"a"],["t",182,53,"CH\u2083\u2014MgCl","ms"],["t",233,65,"CH\u2083"],["l",262,61,283,61],["t",291,65,"C","m"],["l",299,61,320,61],["t",323,65,"CH\u2083"],["l",291,40,291,53],["t",291,33,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",291,69,291,82],["t",285,90,"\u2212","ms"],["t",303,90,"+","ms"],["t",291,103,"O","m"],["t",302,103,"MgCl"],["l",358,60,393,60,"a"],["t",375,53,"H\u2082O","ms"],["t",3,165,"CH\u2083"],["l",32,161,52,161],["t",61,165,"C","m"],["l",69,161,90,161],["t",93,165,"CH\u2083"],["l",61,142,61,155],["t",61,138,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",61,169,61,182],["t",61,196,"OH","m"],["t",138,165,"(Tert. butyl alcohol)"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2020-q148",pn:148,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The s-Block Elements",st:"Biological importance of Na and K",q:"The following metal ion activates many enzymes, participates in the oxidation of glucose to produce ATP and with Na, is responsible for the transmission of nerve signals.",o:["Copper","Calcium","Potassium","Iron"],c:2,s:`Potassium ions activate many enzymes and help in glucose oxidation to give ATP.
With sodium, K\u207A takes part in nerve signal transmission.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The s-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q149",pn:149,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Structure of Atom",st:"Atomic number and mass number",q:"The number of protons, neutrons and electrons in \xB9\u2077\u2075\u2087\u2081Lu, respectively, are :",o:["104, 71 and 71","71, 71 and 104","175, 104 and 71","71, 104 and 71"],c:3,s:`Atomic number 71 gives 71 protons.
Neutrons = 175 \u2212 71 = 104.
The neutral atom has 71 electrons.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Structure of Atom"},{id:"neet-2020-q150",pn:150,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Dipole moment",q:"Which of the following set of molecules will have zero dipole moment ?",o:["Boron trifluoride, hydrogen fluoride, carbon dioxide, 1,3-dichlorobenzene","Nitrogen trifluoride, beryllium difluoride, water, 1,3-dichlorobenzene","Boron trifluoride, beryllium difluoride, carbon dioxide, 1,4-dichlorobenzene","Ammonia, beryllium difluoride, water, 1,4-dichlorobenzene"],c:2,s:`BF\u2083 (trigonal planar), BeF\u2082 and CO\u2082 (linear) and 1,4-dichlorobenzene are symmetrical.
Their bond dipoles cancel, so \u03BC = 0.
HF, NF\u2083, NH\u2083, water and 1,3-dichlorobenzene have net dipole moments.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2020-q151",pn:151,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure",st:"Molecular orbital theory",q:"Identify a molecule which does not exist.",o:["Li\u2082","C\u2082","O\u2082","He\u2082"],c:3,s:`He\u2082 has 4 electrons: \u03C31s\xB2 \u03C3*1s\xB2.
Bond order = \xBD(Nb \u2212 Na) = \xBD(2 \u2212 2) = 0.
Zero bond order means no stable bond, so He\u2082 does not exist.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure"},{id:"neet-2020-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Nomenclature of elements",q:`Identify the incorrect match.
Name  IUPAC Official Name
(a) Unnilunium  (i) Mendelevium
(b) Unniltrium  (ii) Lawrencium
(c) Unnilhexium  (iii) Seaborgium
(d) Unununnium  (iv) Darmstadtium`,o:["(b), (ii)","(c), (iii)","(d), (iv)","(a), (i)"],c:2,s:`Unnilunium = 101 = Mendelevium (correct).
Unniltrium = 103 = Lawrencium (correct).
Unnilhexium = 106 = Seaborgium (correct).
Unununnium = 111 = Roentgenium, not Darmstadtium.
So (d), (iv) is the incorrect match.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2020-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemical Kinetics",st:"First order rate law",q:"The rate constant for a first order reaction is 4.606 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 s\u207B\xB9. The time required to reduce 2.0 g of the reactant to 0.2 g is :",o:["200 s","500 s","1000 s","100 s"],c:1,s:`For first order: t = (2.303/k) log([A]\u2080/[A]).
[A]\u2080/[A] = 2.0/0.2 = 10, so log 10 = 1.
t = 2.303/(4.606 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 500 s.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemical Kinetics"},{id:"neet-2020-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Metallurgy of iron",q:"Identify the correct statement from the following :",o:["Blister copper has blistered appearance due to evolution of CO\u2082","Vapour phase refining is carried out for Nickel by Van Arkel method","Pig iron can be moulded into a variety of shapes","Wrought iron is impure iron with 4% carbon"],c:2,s:`Blister copper looks blistered because SO\u2082 escapes, not CO\u2082.
Nickel is refined by the Mond process; the Van Arkel method is for Zr and Ti.
Pig iron (about 4% carbon) melts and can be cast into moulds.
Wrought iron is the purest form (under 0.5% carbon).
So only (3) is correct.
Note: No metallurgy chapter in the chapter list; nearest chapter used.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Surface Chemistry",st:"Colloids",q:"Measuring Zeta potential is useful in determining which property of colloidal solution ?",o:["Solubility","Stability of the colloidal particles","Size of the colloidal particles","Viscosity"],c:1,s:`Zeta potential is the potential difference across the fixed and diffuse layers of the charge on a colloid particle.
A larger zeta potential means more repulsion between particles, so the sol is more stable.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Surface Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2020-q156",pn:156,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Oxoacids of sulphur",q:"Which of the following oxoacid of sulphur has \u2013 O \u2013 O \u2013 linkage ?",o:["H\u2082SO\u2084, sulphuric acid","H\u2082S\u2082O\u2088, peroxodisulphuric acid","H\u2082S\u2082O\u2087, pyrosulphuric acid","H\u2082SO\u2083, sulphurous acid"],c:1,s:`Peroxodisulphuric acid is HO\u2013SO\u2082\u2013O\u2013O\u2013SO\u2082\u2013OH.
It has a peroxy (\u2013O\u2013O\u2013) link between two sulphur atoms.
The other acids have no O\u2013O bond.
Note: Option 4 is printed as H\u2082SP\u2083 in the paper; written here as H\u2082SO\u2083 (sulphurous acid).`,sfig:{w:245,h:118,alt:"H2S2O8: HO-SO2-O-O-SO2-OH with the O-O group boxed and labelled peroxy linkage",d:[["r",89,30,68,22],["t",8,45,"H","m"],["t",42,45,"O","m"],["t",73,45,"S","m"],["t",105,45,"O","m"],["t",138,45,"O","m"],["t",169,45,"S","m"],["t",200,45,"O","m"],["t",232,45,"H","m"],["l",15,41,35,41],["l",49,41,66,41],["l",80,41,98,41],["l",113,41,130,41],["l",146,41,162,41],["l",176,41,192,41],["l",208,41,224,41],["l",71,21,71,34],["l",75,21,75,34],["t",73,16,"O","m"],["l",71,49,71,62],["l",75,49,75,62],["t",73,76,"O","m"],["l",167,21,167,34],["l",171,21,171,34],["t",169,16,"O","m"],["l",167,49,167,62],["l",171,49,171,62],["t",169,76,"O","m"],["l",122,92,122,56,"a"],["t",122,107,"Peroxy linkage","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q157",pn:157,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes",st:"Elimination reactions",q:`Elimination reaction of 2-Bromo-pentane to form pent-2-ene is :
(a) \u03B2-Elimination reaction
(b) Follows Zaitsev rule
(c) Dehydrohalogenation reaction
(d) Dehydration reaction`,o:["(a), (c), (d)","(b), (c), (d)","(a), (b), (d)","(a), (b), (c)"],c:3,s:`A base (NaOEt) removes H from the \u03B2-carbon and Br leaves from the \u03B1-carbon, so it is \u03B2-elimination (a).
The more substituted alkene pent-2-ene is the major product, which follows Zaitsev rule (b).
Loss of HBr is dehydrohalogenation (c).
Loss of water would be dehydration, so (d) is wrong.`,sfig:{w:390,h:140,alt:"2-Bromopentane with NaOEt (E2): H leaves the beta carbon and Br leaves the alpha carbon, giving pent-2-ene plus EtOH",d:[["t",3,73,"CH\u2083\u2013CH\u2013CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083"],["t",41,56,"\u03B1","m"],["t",65,40,"H","m"],["t",66,92,"\u03B2","m"],["t",40,104,"Br","m"],["l",65,44,65,58],["p","M68 47C75 51 75 58 68 58","a"],["l",40,77,40,91],["p","M37 78C31 85 33 91 38 92","a"],["p","M208 46C212 6 130 -6 74 36","a"],["t",200,52,"+","ms"],["t",212,52,"\u2212","ms"],["l",168,70,248,70,"a"],["t",208,66,"NaOEt","m"],["t",208,86,"E2","m"],["t",255,73,"CH\u2083\u2013CH=CH\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083"],["t",285,93,"+ EtOH"],["t",50,129,"sec. alkyl halide"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Haloalkanes and Haloarenes"},{id:"neet-2020-q158",pn:158,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The p-Block Elements",st:"Carbon and its compounds",q:`Identify the correct statements from the following :
(a) CO\u2082(g) is used as refrigerant for ice-cream and frozen food.
(b) The structure of C\u2086\u2080 contains twelve six carbon rings and twenty five carbon rings.
(c) ZSM-5, a type of zeolite, is used to convert alcohols into gasoline
(d) CO is colourless and odourless gas`,o:["(a) and (c) only","(b) and (c) only","(c) and (d) only","(a), (b) and (c) only"],c:2,s:`(a) Solid CO\u2082 (dry ice) is the refrigerant, not the gas, so (a) is wrong.
(b) C\u2086\u2080 has twenty six-membered and twelve five-membered rings, so (b) is wrong.
(c) ZSM-5 converts alcohols to gasoline - correct.
(d) CO is a colourless, odourless gas - correct.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The p-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q159",pn:159,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Hydrocarbons",st:"Ozonolysis",q:"An alkene on ozonolysis gives methanol as one of the product. Its structure is :",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`Ozonolysis of a =CH\u2082 end gives methanal (HCHO), which is the one-carbon product.
Only option (2), cyclohexyl\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082 with a terminal =CH\u2082, gives methanal and cyclohexyl\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CHO.
Printed key (2) is kept.
Note: Printed key 2 kept; the question says methanol, but ozonolysis gives methanal (HCHO).`,fig:{w:420,h:95,alt:"Four structures: (1) cyclohexene with CH2CH2CH3, (2) cyclohexane with CH2-CH=CH2, (3) cyclohexene with CH2CH2CH3, (4) cyclohexane with CH=CH-CH3",d:[["p","M52.0 42.0L36.4 51.0L36.4 69.0L52.0 78.0L67.6 69.0L67.6 51.0Z"],["l",52,42,52,32],["t",48,27,"CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2082\u2013CH\u2083","s"],["t",6,64,"(1)"],["l",64.2,64.9,64.2,55.1],["p","M152.0 42.0L136.4 51.0L136.4 69.0L152.0 78.0L167.6 69.0L167.6 51.0Z"],["l",152,42,152,32],["t",148,27,"CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082","s"],["t",106,64,"(2)"],["p","M252.0 42.0L236.4 51.0L236.4 69.0L252.0 78.0L267.6 69.0L267.6 51.0Z"],["l",252,42,252,32],["t",248,27,"CH\u2082CH\u2082CH\u2083","s"],["t",206,64,"(3)"],["l",253.8,73,262.3,68.1],["p","M352.0 42.0L336.4 51.0L336.4 69.0L352.0 78.0L367.6 69.0L367.6 51.0Z"],["l",352,42,352,32],["t",348,27,"CH=CH\u2013CH\u2083","s"],["t",306,64,"(4)"]]},sfig:{w:500,h:100,alt:"Cyclohexyl-CH2-CH=CH2 with (i) O3 and (ii) Zn/H2O gives the aldehyde cyclohexyl-CH2-CHO plus methanal, H-C(=O)-H",d:[["p","M32.0 46.0L16.4 55.0L16.4 73.0L32.0 82.0L47.6 73.0L47.6 55.0Z"],["l",32,46,32,30],["t",28,26,"CH\u2082\u2013CH=CH\u2082"],["p","M296.0 46.0L280.4 55.0L280.4 73.0L296.0 82.0L311.6 73.0L311.6 55.0Z"],["l",296,46,296,30],["t",292,26,"CH\u2082\u2013CHO"],["l",134,48,263,48,"a"],["t",198,41,"(i) O\u2083","m"],["t",198,68,"(ii) Zn.H\u2082O","m"],["t",375,62,"+","m"],["t",425,20,"H\u2013C\u2013H"],["l",449,26,449,40],["l",454,26,454,40],["t",451,54,"O","m"],["t",451,76,"Methanal","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Hydrocarbons"},{id:"neet-2020-q160",pn:160,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Chromatography",q:"Paper chromatography is an example of",o:["Partition chromatography","Thin layer chromatography","Column chromatography","Adsorption chromatography"],c:0,s:`In paper chromatography the water held in the paper fibres is the stationary phase.
The components are divided (partitioned) between this water and the moving solvent.
So it is partition chromatography.
Note: The paper prints no solution beyond an NCERT reference; this working is added here.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2020-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties",st:"Nature of oxides",q:`Match the following
Oxide  Nature
(a) CO  (i) Basic
(b) BaO  (ii) Neutral
(c) Al\u2082O\u2083  (iii) Acidic
(d) Cl\u2082O\u2087  (iv) Amphoteric`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)"],c:0,s:`CO is a neutral oxide.
BaO is a basic oxide (metal oxide of group 2).
Al\u2082O\u2083 is amphoteric.
Cl\u2082O\u2087 is an acidic oxide (non-metal, high oxidation state).
Note: The paper numbers the oxides (1)-(4); written here as (a)-(d) to match the option columns.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties"},{id:"neet-2020-q162",pn:162,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry",st:"Mole concept",q:"Which one of the followings has maximum number of atoms?",o:["1 g of Mg(s) [Atomic mass of Mg = 24]","1 g of O\u2082(g) [Atomic mass of O = 16]","1 g of Li(s) [Atomic mass of Li = 7]","1 g of Ag(s) [Atomic mass of Ag = 108]"],c:2,s:`Number of atoms = (mass/molar mass) \xD7 N_A \xD7 atoms per molecule.
Mg: 1/24 N_A.
O\u2082: (1/32) \xD7 2 N_A = 1/16 N_A.
Li: 1/7 N_A.
Ag: 1/108 N_A.
1/7 is the largest, so Li has the most atoms.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Some Basic Concepts of Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2020-q163",pn:163,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Amino acids",q:"Which of the following is a basic amino acid ?",o:["Alanine","Tyrosine","Lysine","Serine"],c:2,s:`Lysine has two \u2013NH\u2082 groups (one in the side chain) and one \u2013COOH.
More amino groups than carboxyl groups make it a basic amino acid.`,sfig:{w:180,h:150,alt:"Lysine in Fischer form: COOH on top, NH2 left and H right of the central carbon, (CH2)4-NH2 at the bottom, with lone pairs on both nitrogens",d:[["t",80,16,"COOH","m"],["l",65,24,65,96],["l",32,57,103,57],["t",2,62,"NH\u2082"],["c",8,50,1,"F"],["c",13,50,1,"F"],["t",110,62,"H"],["t",56,108,"(CH\u2082)\u2084\u2013NH\u2082"],["c",113,96,1,"F"],["c",118,96,1,"F"],["t",72,138,"Lysine","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2020-q164",pn:164,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Magnetic moment",q:"The calculated spin only magnetic moment of Cr\xB2\u207A ion is",o:["4.90 BM","5.92 BM","2.84 BM","3.87 BM"],c:0,s:`Cr has 3d\u20754s\xB9; Cr\xB2\u207A is 3d\u2074.
Four unpaired electrons, n = 4.
\u03BC = \u221A(n(n+2)) = \u221A24 = 4.90 BM.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q165",pn:165,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)",st:"Carbohydrates",q:"Sucrose on hydrolysis gives",o:["\u03B1-D-Glucose + \u03B2-D-Glucose","\u03B1-D-Glucose + \u03B2-D-Fructose","\u03B1-D-Fructose + \u03B2-D-Fructose","\u03B2-D-Glucose + \u03B1-D-Fructose"],c:1,s:`Sucrose is made of \u03B1-D-glucose and \u03B2-D-fructose joined through C1 and C2.
Hydrolysis (with H\u2083O\u207A) splits it back into these two.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Biomolecules (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2020-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Solutions",st:"Deviation from Raoult's law",q:"The mixture which shows positive deviation from Raoult's law is :",o:["Benzene + Toluene","Acetone + Chloroform","Chloroethane + Bromoethane","Ethanol + Acetone"],c:3,s:`Benzene + toluene and chloroethane + bromoethane are nearly ideal.
Acetone + chloroform forms hydrogen bonds: negative deviation.
Acetone breaks the hydrogen bonds of ethanol, so the interactions weaken and vapour pressure rises: positive deviation.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2020-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques",st:"Carbocation stability",q:"A tertiary butyl carbocation is more stable than a secondary butyl carbocation because of which of the following ?",o:["+ R effect of \u2013 CH\u2083 groups","\u2013 R effect of \u2013 CH\u2083 groups","Hyperconjugation","\u2013 I effect of \u2013 CH\u2083 groups"],c:2,s:`The tert-butyl cation has 9 C\u2013H bonds on its adjacent carbons; the sec-butyl cation has fewer.
More C\u2013H bonds give more hyperconjugation structures and more stability.`,sfig:{w:360,h:135,alt:"tert-butyl cation (CH3)3C+ with 9 alpha hydrogens is shown more stable than the sec-butyl cation CH3-CH(+)-CH2-CH3",d:[["t",70,14,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",66,20,66,38],["t",10,59,"CH\u2083"],["l",40,55,59,55],["t",66,59,"C","m"],["t",73,50,"\u2295","s"],["l",66,66,66,84],["t",70,100,"CH\u2083","m"],["t",66,125,"(9\u03B1 H)","m"],["t",130,59,">","m"],["t",162,59,"CH\u2083"],["l",190,55,212,55],["t",226,59,"CH","m"],["t",226,40,"\u2295","ms"],["l",240,55,260,55],["t",264,59,"CH\u2082"],["l",293,55,315,55],["t",320,59,"CH\u2083"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Organic Chemistry: Some Basic Principles and Techniques"},{id:"neet-2020-q168",pn:168,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Solubility product",q:"Find out the solubility of Ni(OH)\u2082 in 0.1 M NaOH. Given that the ionic product of Ni(OH)\u2082 is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075",o:["2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 M","1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M","1 \xD7 10\u2078 M","2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M"],c:3,s:`NaOH gives 0.1 M OH\u207B.
Let the solubility of Ni(OH)\u2082 be S: [Ni\xB2\u207A] = S, [OH\u207B] = 0.1 + 2S \u2248 0.1.
K = S(0.1)\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075.
S = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075/10\u207B\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 M.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2020-q169",pn:169,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Chemistry in Everyday Life",st:"Detergents",q:"Which of the following is a cationic detergent ?",o:["Sodium stearate","Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide","Sodium dodecylbenzene sulphonate","Sodium lauryl sulphate"],c:1,s:`Cationic detergents have a positively charged quaternary ammonium head.
Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide is such a detergent.
The others are anionic (sulphonate, sulphate or carboxylate).
Note: Option 2 is printed as Catyltrimethyl ammonium bromide; written here as the correct name.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Chemistry in Everyday Life"},{id:"neet-2020-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Solutions",st:"Depression in freezing point",q:"The freezing point depression constant (K_f) of benzene is 5.12 K kg mol\u207B\xB9. The freezing point depression for the solution of molality 0.078 m containing a non-electrolyte solute in benzene is (rounded off upto two decimal places)",o:["0.80 K","0.40 K","0.90 K","0.20 K"],c:1,s:`\u0394T_f = K_f \xD7 m.
= 5.12 \xD7 0.078 = 0.399 K.
Rounded to two places this is 0.40 K.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Solutions"},{id:"neet-2020-q171",pn:171,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"The d- and f-Block Elements",st:"Transition metals",q:"Identify the incorrect statement",o:["The transition metals and their compounds are known for their catalytic activity due to their ability to adopt multiple oxidation states and to form complexes","Interstitial compounds are those that are formed when small atom like H, C or N are trapped inside the crystal lattices of metals","The oxidation states of chromium in CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B and Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B are not the same","Cr\xB2\u207A(d\u2074) is a stronger reducing agent than Fe\xB2\u207A(d\u2076) in water"],c:2,s:`In CrO\u2084\xB2\u207B: x + 4(\u22122) = \u22122, so x = +6.
In Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B: 2x + 7(\u22122) = \u22122, so x = +6.
The oxidation states are the same, so statement (3) is incorrect.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"The d- and f-Block Elements"},{id:"neet-2020-q172",pn:172,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Environmental Chemistry",st:"Carbon monoxide pollution",q:"Which of the following is not correct about carbon monoxide ?",o:["It reduces oxygen carrying ability of blood","The carboxyhaemoglobin (haemoglobin bound to CO) is less stable than oxyhaemoglobin","It is produced due to incomplete combustion","It forms carboxyhaemoglobin"],c:1,s:`CO binds haemoglobin about 200 times more strongly than O\u2082.
So carboxyhaemoglobin is more stable than oxyhaemoglobin, and statement (2) is wrong.
The other statements are true.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Environmental Chemistry"},{id:"neet-2020-q173",pn:173,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"Gibbs energy and equilibrium",q:"Hydrolysis of sucrose is given by the following reaction. Sucrose + H\u2082O \u21CC Glucose + Fructose. If the equilibrium constant (K_c) is 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3 at 300 K, the value of \u0394_rG\xB0 at the same temperature will be",o:["8.314 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 \xD7 300 K \xD7 ln(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3)","8.314 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 \xD7 300 K \xD7 ln(3 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3)","\u20138.314 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 \xD7 300 K \xD7 ln(4 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3)","\u20138.314 J mol\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9 \xD7 300 K \xD7 ln(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3)"],c:3,s:`\u0394_rG\xB0 = \u2212RT ln K.
K = 2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3, T = 300 K.
\u0394_rG\xB0 = \u22128.314 \xD7 300 \xD7 ln(2 \xD7 10\xB9\xB3).`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2020-q174",pn:174,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Coordination Compounds",st:"Spectrochemical series",q:"Which of the following is the correct order of increasing field strength of ligands to form coordination compounds ?",o:["SCN\u207B < F\u207B < CN\u207B < C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B","F\u207B < SCN\u207B < C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B < CN\u207B","CN\u207B < C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B < SCN\u207B < F\u207B","SCN\u207B < F\u207B < C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B < CN\u207B"],c:3,s:"The spectrochemical series in increasing field strength is SCN\u207B < F\u207B < C\u2082O\u2084\xB2\u207B < CN\u207B (weak to strong).",sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Coordination Compounds"},{id:"neet-2020-q175",pn:175,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids",st:"Preparation of aldehydes",q:"Identify compound X in the following sequence of reactions : toluene \u2192 (Cl\u2082/h\u03BD) X \u2192 (H\u2082O, 373 K) benzaldehyde",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`Chlorine in light replaces two H of the CH\u2083 group: C\u2086H\u2085CHCl\u2082 (benzal chloride).
Hydrolysis with water at 373 K gives the unstable gem-diol, which loses water to form C\u2086H\u2085CHO.
So X is C\u2086H\u2085CHCl\u2082.`,fig:{w:440,h:175,alt:"Toluene reacts with Cl2/hv to give X, and X with H2O at 373 K gives benzaldehyde; options (1) benzyl chloride C6H5CH2Cl, (2) C6H5CHCl2, (3) C6H5CCl3, (4) chlorobenzene",d:[["p","M25.0 38.0L7.7 48.0L7.7 68.0L25.0 78.0L42.3 68.0L42.3 48.0Z"],["l",11.8,52.8,11.8,63.2],["l",27.1,72,36.1,66.8],["l",36.1,49.2,27.1,44],["l",25,38,25,26],["t",31,22,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",63,56,104,56,"a"],["t",84,47,"Cl\u2082/h\u03BD","ms"],["t",118,60,"X","m"],["l",132,56,182,56,"a"],["t",157,47,"H\u2082O","ms"],["t",157,69,"373 K","ms"],["p","M207.0 38.0L189.7 48.0L189.7 68.0L207.0 78.0L224.3 68.0L224.3 48.0Z"],["l",193.8,52.8,193.8,63.2],["l",209.1,72,218.1,66.8],["l",218.1,49.2,209.1,44],["l",207,38,207,26],["t",212,22,"CHO","m"],["p","M46.0 122.0L28.7 132.0L28.7 152.0L46.0 162.0L63.3 152.0L63.3 132.0Z"],["l",32.8,136.8,32.8,147.2],["l",48.1,156,57.1,150.8],["l",57.1,133.2,48.1,128],["l",46,122,46,110],["t",41,106,"CH\u2082Cl"],["t",7,146,"(1)"],["p","M160.0 122.0L142.7 132.0L142.7 152.0L160.0 162.0L177.3 152.0L177.3 132.0Z"],["l",146.8,136.8,146.8,147.2],["l",162.1,156,171.1,150.8],["l",171.1,133.2,162.1,128],["l",160,122,160,110],["t",155,106,"CHCl\u2082"],["t",121,146,"(2)"],["p","M274.0 122.0L256.7 132.0L256.7 152.0L274.0 162.0L291.3 152.0L291.3 132.0Z"],["l",260.8,136.8,260.8,147.2],["l",276.1,156,285.1,150.8],["l",285.1,133.2,276.1,128],["l",274,122,274,110],["t",269,106,"CCl\u2083"],["t",235,146,"(3)"],["p","M388.0 122.0L370.7 132.0L370.7 152.0L388.0 162.0L405.3 152.0L405.3 132.0Z"],["l",374.8,136.8,374.8,147.2],["l",390.1,156,399.1,150.8],["l",399.1,133.2,390.1,128],["l",388,122,388,110],["t",383,106,"Cl"],["t",349,146,"(4)"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:100,alt:"Toluene with 2Cl2 and light gives benzal chloride C6H5CHCl2, which with H2O at 373 K gives benzaldehyde",d:[["p","M25.0 40.0L7.7 50.0L7.7 70.0L25.0 80.0L42.3 70.0L42.3 50.0Z"],["l",11.8,54.8,11.8,65.2],["l",27.1,74,36.1,68.8],["l",36.1,51.2,27.1,46],["l",25,40,25,28],["t",30,24,"CH\u2083","m"],["l",63,58,112,58,"a"],["t",88,52,"2Cl\u2082","ms"],["t",88,72,"h\u03BD","ms"],["p","M150.0 40.0L132.7 50.0L132.7 70.0L150.0 80.0L167.3 70.0L167.3 50.0Z"],["l",136.8,54.8,136.8,65.2],["l",152.1,74,161.1,68.8],["l",161.1,51.2,152.1,46],["l",150,40,150,28],["t",150,24,"CH","m"],["l",157,28,170,15],["l",160,32,170,44],["t",172,15,"Cl"],["t",172,52,"Cl"],["l",188,58,236,58,"a"],["t",212,52,"H\u2082O","ms"],["t",212,72,"373 K","ms"],["p","M262.0 40.0L244.7 50.0L244.7 70.0L262.0 80.0L279.3 70.0L279.3 50.0Z"],["l",248.8,54.8,248.8,65.2],["l",264.1,74,273.1,68.8],["l",273.1,51.2,264.1,46],["l",262,40,262,28],["t",262,24,"CHO","m"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Aldehydes, Ketones and Carboxylic Acids"},{id:"neet-2020-q176",pn:176,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)",st:"First law",q:"The correct option for free expansion of an ideal gas under adiabatic condition is.",o:["q = 0, \u0394T < 0 and w > 0","q < 0, \u0394T = 0 and w = 0","q > 0, \u0394T > 0 and w > 0","q = 0, \u0394T = 0 and w = 0"],c:3,s:`In free expansion the external pressure is zero, so w = 0.
Adiabatic means q = 0.
\u0394U = q + w = 0.
For an ideal gas \u0394U = nC_v\u0394T = 0, so \u0394T = 0.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Thermodynamics (Chemistry)"},{id:"neet-2020-q177",pn:177,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Electrochemistry",st:"Faraday's laws",q:"The number of Faradays (F) required to produce 20 g of calcium from molten CaCl\u2082 (Atomic mass of Ca = 40 g mol\u207B\xB9) is :",o:["2","3","4","1"],c:3,s:`Ca\xB2\u207A + 2e\u207B \u2192 Ca, so 2 F deposit 1 mol (40 g).
Moles in 20 g = 20/40 = 0.5.
Charge = 0.5 \xD7 2 = 1 F.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Electrochemistry"},{id:"neet-2020-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Equilibrium",st:"Common ion effect",q:"HCl was passed through solution of CaCl\u2082, MgCl\u2082 and NaCl. Which of the following compound(s) crystallise(s) ?",o:["Only NaCl","Only MgCl\u2082","NaCl, MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082","Both MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082"],c:0,s:`HCl adds Cl\u207B ions, raising [Cl\u207B].
The ionic product [Na\u207A][Cl\u207B] first exceeds the solubility product of NaCl, so NaCl separates (common ion effect).
MgCl\u2082 and CaCl\u2082 are much more soluble and stay in solution.`,sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Equilibrium"},{id:"neet-2020-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers",st:"Cleavage of ethers",q:"Anisole on cleavage with HI gives:",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:3,s:`HI protonates the oxygen of anisole.
I\u207B attacks the smaller CH\u2083 group by SN2 (the phenyl C\u2013O bond is too strong to break).
Products: phenol + CH\u2083I.`,fig:{w:330,h:205,alt:"Four product pairs: (1) iodobenzene + CH3OH, (2) phenol + C2H5I, (3) iodobenzene + C2H5OH, (4) phenol + CH3I",d:[["p","M50.0 38.0L30.9 49.0L30.9 71.0L50.0 82.0L69.1 71.0L69.1 49.0Z"],["l",35.5,54.3,35.5,65.7],["l",52.3,75.4,62.2,69.7],["l",62.2,50.3,52.3,44.6],["l",50,38,50,26],["t",50,22,"I","m"],["t",77,64,"+ CH\u2083OH"],["t",4,64,"(1)"],["p","M215.0 38.0L195.9 49.0L195.9 71.0L215.0 82.0L234.1 71.0L234.1 49.0Z"],["l",200.5,54.3,200.5,65.7],["l",217.3,75.4,227.2,69.7],["l",227.2,50.3,217.3,44.6],["l",215,38,215,26],["t",215,22,"OH","m"],["t",242,64,"+ C\u2082H\u2085I"],["t",169,64,"(2)"],["p","M50.0 138.0L30.9 149.0L30.9 171.0L50.0 182.0L69.1 171.0L69.1 149.0Z"],["l",35.5,154.3,35.5,165.7],["l",52.3,175.4,62.2,169.7],["l",62.2,150.3,52.3,144.6],["l",50,138,50,126],["t",50,122,"I","m"],["t",77,164,"+ C\u2082H\u2085OH"],["t",4,164,"(3)"],["p","M215.0 138.0L195.9 149.0L195.9 171.0L215.0 182.0L234.1 171.0L234.1 149.0Z"],["l",200.5,154.3,200.5,165.7],["l",217.3,175.4,227.2,169.7],["l",227.2,150.3,217.3,144.6],["l",215,138,215,126],["t",215,122,"OH","m"],["t",242,164,"+ CH\u2083I"],["t",169,164,"(4)"]]},sfig:{w:400,h:125,alt:"Anisole is protonated by HI to the oxonium ion; I- attacks the CH3 group by SN2 giving phenol + CH3I",d:[["p","M40.0 68.0L20.9 79.0L20.9 101.0L40.0 112.0L59.1 101.0L59.1 79.0Z"],["l",25.5,84.3,25.5,95.7],["l",42.3,105.4,52.2,99.7],["l",52.2,80.3,42.3,74.6],["l",40,68,40,58],["t",40,54,"O","m"],["c",30,45,1,"F"],["c",30,50,1,"F"],["l",46,50,54,50],["t",56,54,"CH\u2083"],["p","M80 42C100 38 104 52 97 62","a"],["t",79,76,"+\u03B4","s"],["t",96,76,"\u2212\u03B4","s"],["t",90,88,"H\u2013I","m"],["l",70,100,125,100,"a"],["p","M155.0 68.0L135.9 79.0L135.9 101.0L155.0 112.0L174.1 101.0L174.1 79.0Z"],["l",140.5,84.3,140.5,95.7],["l",157.3,105.4,167.2,99.7],["l",167.2,80.3,157.3,74.6],["l",174,79,184,70],["t",188,68,"O","m"],["t",188,52,"\u2295","ms"],["l",188,72,188,84],["t",188,96,"H","m"],["l",194,64,201,64],["t",203,68,"CH\u2083"],["p","M243,76C247,58 235,50 227,58","a"],["t",243,88,"I\u2296","m"],["l",218,100,275,100,"a"],["t",246,116,"SN2","m"],["p","M310.0 68.0L290.9 79.0L290.9 101.0L310.0 112.0L329.1 101.0L329.1 79.0Z"],["l",295.5,84.3,295.5,95.7],["l",312.3,105.4,322.2,99.7],["l",322.2,80.3,312.3,74.6],["l",310,68,310,58],["t",310,54,"OH","m"],["t",338,94,"+ CH\u2083\u2013I"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Alcohols, Phenols and Ethers"},{id:"neet-2020-q180",pn:180,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Amines",st:"Carbylamine test",q:"Which of the following amine will give the carbylamine test ?",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:3,s:`Only primary amines give the carbylamine (isocyanide) test with CHCl\u2083 and KOH.
Option (4), aniline (C\u2086H\u2085NH\u2082), is primary.
The N-methyl, N-ethyl and N,N-dimethyl compounds are secondary or tertiary.`,fig:{w:440,h:85,alt:"Four substituted benzenes: (1) N-methylaniline NHCH3, (2) N,N-dimethylaniline N(CH3)2, (3) N-ethylaniline NHC2H5, (4) aniline NH2",d:[["p","M45.0 32.0L27.7 42.0L27.7 62.0L45.0 72.0L62.3 62.0L62.3 42.0Z"],["l",31.8,46.8,31.8,57.2],["l",47.1,66,56.1,60.8],["l",56.1,43.2,47.1,38],["l",45,32,45,22],["t",40,18,"NHCH\u2083"],["t",7,56,"(1)"],["p","M160.0 32.0L142.7 42.0L142.7 62.0L160.0 72.0L177.3 62.0L177.3 42.0Z"],["l",146.8,46.8,146.8,57.2],["l",162.1,66,171.1,60.8],["l",171.1,43.2,162.1,38],["l",160,32,160,22],["t",155,18,"N(CH\u2083)\u2082"],["t",122,56,"(2)"],["p","M275.0 32.0L257.7 42.0L257.7 62.0L275.0 72.0L292.3 62.0L292.3 42.0Z"],["l",261.8,46.8,261.8,57.2],["l",277.1,66,286.1,60.8],["l",286.1,43.2,277.1,38],["l",275,32,275,22],["t",270,18,"NHC\u2082H\u2085"],["t",237,56,"(3)"],["p","M390.0 32.0L372.7 42.0L372.7 62.0L390.0 72.0L407.3 62.0L407.3 42.0Z"],["l",376.8,46.8,376.8,57.2],["l",392.1,66,401.1,60.8],["l",401.1,43.2,392.1,38],["l",390,32,390,22],["t",385,18,"NH\u2082"],["t",352,56,"(4)"]]},sub:"Chemistry",ch:"Amines"}]})();
